1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,520

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:07,840
OK, let's get started.

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:08,580
Cool.

4
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:11,080
So as a recap of
last week, we talked

5
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:13,833
about multi-task
reinforcement learning

6
00:00:13,833 --> 00:00:16,000
and how we might want to
have reinforcement learning

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,637
agents not learn just one task,
but multiple different things

8
00:00:19,637 --> 00:00:21,720
that might correspond to
multiple different reward

9
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:22,840
functions.

10
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:29,080
And we saw that we can
formulate an aggregated MDP that

11
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:32,479
encapsulates all of the
different tasks, where you now

12
00:00:32,479 --> 00:00:36,620
are augmenting your state with
some sort of task descriptor,

13
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:42,800
like a prompt or a goal or just
an index corresponding to what

14
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:44,540
the agent should do.

15
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:46,040
And the way that
this worked is we'd

16
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:51,600
sample some task, run the
policy in the MDP for that task,

17
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:54,120
sample another
task, and so forth.

18
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:57,960
And we would then
get a policy that

19
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:03,430
could be able to successfully
achieve high reward

20
00:01:03,430 --> 00:01:05,069
in a number of different MDPs.

21
00:01:05,069 --> 00:01:08,070
Or another way to
view it, as single,

22
00:01:08,070 --> 00:01:12,060
aggregated MDP with
this augmented state.

23
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:14,197

24
00:01:14,197 --> 00:01:16,530
This is going to be a little
bit different from meta-RL.

25
00:01:16,530 --> 00:01:21,230
So we'll see some differences
here later in this lecture.

26
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:23,190
And we also somewhat
briefly talked

27
00:01:23,190 --> 00:01:25,510
about goal-conditioned
reinforcement learning.

28
00:01:25,510 --> 00:01:27,830
And it could just
be simply viewed

29
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:30,910
as a special case of multi-task
reinforcement learning,

30
00:01:30,910 --> 00:01:34,630
where each task is trying
to reach a goal state.

31
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:37,890
And the task descriptor
corresponds to that goal state.

32
00:01:37,890 --> 00:01:40,010
And so if your states
are images, for example,

33
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:42,550
then now you'll be
passing as input

34
00:01:42,550 --> 00:01:46,450
to the policy, both the current
image and the goal image.

35
00:01:46,450 --> 00:01:48,470
And you'll be trying to
reach that goal image.

36
00:01:48,470 --> 00:01:50,930
Or if your state, for
example, is a vector,

37
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:52,990
you'll be passing
in two vectors.

38
00:01:52,990 --> 00:01:56,470
And the task is trying
to reach some goal state.

39
00:01:56,470 --> 00:01:58,710
And you can construct
different reward functions,

40
00:01:58,710 --> 00:02:01,090
if you have discrete states
or continuous states,

41
00:02:01,090 --> 00:02:04,210
as some sort of, did
you reach that goal

42
00:02:04,210 --> 00:02:06,690
or reach close to that goal.

43
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:08,810
And one of the
things that's kind

44
00:02:08,810 --> 00:02:11,290
of nice about this
goal-conditioned formulation

45
00:02:11,290 --> 00:02:13,770
of reinforcement learning
is that you can actually

46
00:02:13,770 --> 00:02:16,177
do it in a self-supervised way.

47
00:02:16,177 --> 00:02:17,510
You don't need task descriptors.

48
00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:19,330
You don't need a
different set of tasks

49
00:02:19,330 --> 00:02:20,910
that you've manually defined.

50
00:02:20,910 --> 00:02:23,650
You can just use a bunch
of different states

51
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:26,530
as the tasks themselves.

52
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:29,010
And a lot of different tasks
can be formulated as goal

53
00:02:29,010 --> 00:02:31,210
reaching tasks.

54
00:02:31,210 --> 00:02:34,210
One thing to note here is it
can be pretty hard to train.

55
00:02:34,210 --> 00:02:37,770
But you'll be experimenting
with a simple, goal-conditioned

56
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:42,290
reinforcement learning
setting in your homework.

57
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:44,690
OK, so for today we're going
to be talking about meta

58
00:02:44,690 --> 00:02:45,870
reinforcement learning.

59
00:02:45,870 --> 00:02:48,530
And specifically, we'll be
talking about what that problem

60
00:02:48,530 --> 00:02:53,090
statement looks like and
a class of meta-RL methods

61
00:02:53,090 --> 00:02:56,760
that I like to refer to as
black-box meta-RL methods.

62
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:59,760
We'll start to get into
what these methods look like

63
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:03,040
and also some of the
challenges that they face.

64
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:04,340
So what's the problem?

65
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:07,760
Why do we care about meta-RL?

66
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:09,540
Why not just do vanilla RL?

67
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:12,280
So say that you're
trying to figure out

68
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,720
how to operate an espresso
machine that you've never

69
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:16,270
used before.

70
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:19,720

71
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:23,220
If you wanted to do
that, well, first,

72
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:26,160
if you wanted a robot
or a reinforcement

73
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:28,400
learning agent to do
that, it would probably

74
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:30,840
take a lot of data,
a lot of attempts

75
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:33,995
to try to figure out
how to make coffee.

76
00:03:33,995 --> 00:03:35,620
Because it's a pretty
complicated task,

77
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:37,600
lots of different
steps involved.

78
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:39,720
You have to do the
steps in sequence.

79
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:43,200
And also, the reward signal that
you get for this sort of task

80
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:46,120
probably isn't going
to be super detailed.

81
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:49,880
But as a human, if you
were to attempt this task,

82
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:52,760
probably if you had a
little bit of instruction

83
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:55,470
or some previous
experience making espresso,

84
00:03:55,470 --> 00:03:58,470
then you could probably learn it
in minutes, or maybe even less

85
00:03:58,470 --> 00:04:01,110
than a minute.

86
00:04:01,110 --> 00:04:04,270
So if you think about
reinforcement learning, this gap

87
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:07,750
between how quickly a
human could learn something

88
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:11,190
and how slowly it would take a
reinforcement learning system

89
00:04:11,190 --> 00:04:15,150
to learn it, this is
rather unsatisfying.

90
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:18,630
And if we think about what is
the difference between a person

91
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:21,362
and a reinforcement
learning system,

92
00:04:21,362 --> 00:04:23,070
one of the big
differences is that people

93
00:04:23,070 --> 00:04:25,150
aren't starting from scratch.

94
00:04:25,150 --> 00:04:29,550
People already know how to move
objects around with their hands.

95
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:32,190
They might already know how
to make coffee in other ways

96
00:04:32,190 --> 00:04:33,750
or with other machines.

97
00:04:33,750 --> 00:04:36,990
And they can leverage
that experience and also

98
00:04:36,990 --> 00:04:39,678
some notion of semantic
knowledge of what

99
00:04:39,678 --> 00:04:41,470
to do if someone tells
you to pick up a cup

100
00:04:41,470 --> 00:04:44,170
and put it somewhere,
or to turn a knob.

101
00:04:44,170 --> 00:04:46,670
And I can leverage that
previous experience in order

102
00:04:46,670 --> 00:04:50,070
to solve this problem
really, really quickly,

103
00:04:50,070 --> 00:04:52,830
compared to training a
reinforcement learning system

104
00:04:52,830 --> 00:04:53,460
from scratch.

105
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:56,330

106
00:04:56,330 --> 00:04:58,650
And so experience
with similar tasks,

107
00:04:58,650 --> 00:05:02,250
other espresso-making machines,
either espresso making

108
00:05:02,250 --> 00:05:04,580
on other machines or just
general motor control,

109
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:06,330
following instructions,
that sort of thing

110
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:09,890
can be very useful when
trying to solve a new task.

111
00:05:09,890 --> 00:05:13,390
And I mentioned this as kind of
an example with espresso making,

112
00:05:13,390 --> 00:05:17,090
but this framework of
not starting from scratch

113
00:05:17,090 --> 00:05:18,430
is really general.

114
00:05:18,430 --> 00:05:21,370
So it applies to
robotics problems

115
00:05:21,370 --> 00:05:23,490
where you're facing
a new environment

116
00:05:23,490 --> 00:05:25,330
or facing a new task.

117
00:05:25,330 --> 00:05:28,890
But it's also really faced by
many reinforcement learning

118
00:05:28,890 --> 00:05:29,730
systems.

119
00:05:29,730 --> 00:05:32,450
As another example, say you
want to solve a challenging math

120
00:05:32,450 --> 00:05:34,690
problem that you
haven't solved before.

121
00:05:34,690 --> 00:05:38,090
If you have experience solving
previous math problems,

122
00:05:38,090 --> 00:05:40,548
then you can use that
to more quickly solve

123
00:05:40,548 --> 00:05:43,090
a new problem, because you've
learned some sort of strategies

124
00:05:43,090 --> 00:05:47,610
that you might be able to
apply to the new scenario.

125
00:05:47,610 --> 00:05:49,170
OK, we're framing
this question of,

126
00:05:49,170 --> 00:05:52,120
can reinforcement learning
algorithms leverage experience

127
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:56,680
from previous tasks in order
to learn a new task with less

128
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:59,920
data and less experience?

129
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:01,813
In many ways, this
problem is thought

130
00:06:01,813 --> 00:06:03,480
of as a transfer
learning problem, where

131
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:06,700
we want to transfer
knowledge, information,

132
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:12,610
experience from previous
tasks to new tasks.

133
00:06:12,610 --> 00:06:14,360
And there are a few
different formulations

134
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:16,400
and a few different
problem settings

135
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:19,540
that the research community
has thought about.

136
00:06:19,540 --> 00:06:24,760
One is a fairly standard,
a fairly simple setup

137
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:27,320
where you learn one task and
then want to learn a new task.

138
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:29,320
And you can just, for
example, train on one task

139
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:31,340
and then fine-tune
on the new task.

140
00:06:31,340 --> 00:06:34,920
And notions of fine-tuning that
we use in supervised learning

141
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:38,212
are also applicable to
reinforcement learning settings,

142
00:06:38,212 --> 00:06:40,420
where you could fine-tune
a Q-function to a new task,

143
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:43,280
or fine-tune a
policy to a new task.

144
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:47,400
Although, for this to
work well, the tasks

145
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:51,222
need to be quite
similar to each other.

146
00:06:51,222 --> 00:06:52,930
We also talked about
multi-task learning.

147
00:06:52,930 --> 00:06:55,350
And you could also use
multi-task learning for transfer

148
00:06:55,350 --> 00:06:57,070
to new tasks.

149
00:06:57,070 --> 00:07:03,830
And in particular, if your
prompt shares some structure,

150
00:07:03,830 --> 00:07:07,190
if it's just like a
task 1, task 2, task 3;

151
00:07:07,190 --> 00:07:08,730
transfer is going to be harder.

152
00:07:08,730 --> 00:07:10,850
But if it's something that
has a lot of structure,

153
00:07:10,850 --> 00:07:13,250
like pick up a water
bottle, pick up a phone,

154
00:07:13,250 --> 00:07:18,030
pick up a pen, and so forth;
then you might actually

155
00:07:18,030 --> 00:07:21,110
be able to have
things transfer what's

156
00:07:21,110 --> 00:07:23,150
called zero-shot, without
any new experience

157
00:07:23,150 --> 00:07:26,790
at all, simply by passing
that prompt to the system

158
00:07:26,790 --> 00:07:32,470
and having it act
according to that prompt.

159
00:07:32,470 --> 00:07:35,870
And then meta-learning is
often concerned with, instead

160
00:07:35,870 --> 00:07:38,790
of zero-shot transfer, something
that's called few-shot transfer,

161
00:07:38,790 --> 00:07:40,850
where with a little
bit of experience,

162
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:44,110
a few examples or a
few trials of a task,

163
00:07:44,110 --> 00:07:47,770
can you adapt to that task?

164
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:50,967
And essentially the difference
between multi-task learning

165
00:07:50,967 --> 00:07:53,050
and meta-learning is that
it accounts for the fact

166
00:07:53,050 --> 00:07:54,890
that we're going to
be trying to adapt

167
00:07:54,890 --> 00:07:57,330
with a small amount of data.

168
00:07:57,330 --> 00:08:01,610
And it does that during the
original training process.

169
00:08:01,610 --> 00:08:03,210
And another way of
thinking about this

170
00:08:03,210 --> 00:08:05,530
is that instead of having
the task descriptor be

171
00:08:05,530 --> 00:08:08,890
a prompt or an index, the
task descriptor might actually

172
00:08:08,890 --> 00:08:11,970
be a dataset itself,
like a set of trials

173
00:08:11,970 --> 00:08:15,010
that you're going
to be adapting to

174
00:08:15,010 --> 00:08:17,560
in the context of some policy.

175
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:19,810
Another word for this is
sometimes in-context learning

176
00:08:19,810 --> 00:08:21,690
or few-shot learning.

177
00:08:21,690 --> 00:08:24,070
Now, for this to work, for
the first scenario to work,

178
00:08:24,070 --> 00:08:27,250
you must have very similar
source and target tasks.

179
00:08:27,250 --> 00:08:30,810
Whereas, in some ways, things
get a little bit easier

180
00:08:30,810 --> 00:08:33,890
and a little bit harder
for number 2 and number 3.

181
00:08:33,890 --> 00:08:36,090
They get easier in the
sense that you no longer

182
00:08:36,090 --> 00:08:37,830
have to have an
extremely similar task.

183
00:08:37,830 --> 00:08:40,850
You, instead, just need to
have a distribution of tasks

184
00:08:40,850 --> 00:08:44,650
and be similar to that
distribution, such

185
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:47,320
that conceivably, the new task
that you're trying to solve

186
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:49,720
should be within the
distribution of the task

187
00:08:49,720 --> 00:08:51,835
that you've seen
during training.

188
00:08:51,835 --> 00:08:53,960
It gets a little bit harder
because you do actually

189
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:58,720
need a number of training tasks,
not just a single training task,

190
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:02,360
in order to get
effective transfer.

191
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:05,480
OK, before we jump into
meta-RL, let's just

192
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:08,080
look at a couple few-shot
learning examples outside of RL,

193
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:11,280
and then go back
to the RL setting.

194
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:12,800
So actually, I
picked this example

195
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:13,900
so that it's not trivial.

196
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:16,840
There's other test data points
that are much, much easier.

197
00:09:16,840 --> 00:09:17,500
Cool.

198
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:19,960
So that's the essence of a
few-shot image classification

199
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:23,340
problem, where you have a
small number of data points.

200
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:26,920
And your goal is to
classify a new data point.

201
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:30,080
And typically, if you were to
train from scratch on just six

202
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:32,360
images, you'd have
no hope of being

203
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:34,440
able to learn a
classifier, especially

204
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:35,968
on high-dimensional images.

205
00:09:35,968 --> 00:09:37,760
But the way that
meta-learning works is you

206
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:41,280
can construct other tasks for
the machine learning system

207
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:43,340
to solve that have
a similar structure,

208
00:09:43,340 --> 00:09:45,630
like other image
classification tasks,

209
00:09:45,630 --> 00:09:48,790
train it to be able to take
as input a training dataset

210
00:09:48,790 --> 00:09:55,270
and a query image or a test
input, and then predict a label.

211
00:09:55,270 --> 00:09:58,390
And basically, constructing
each of these few-shot learning

212
00:09:58,390 --> 00:10:03,110
problems as a data
point themselves.

213
00:10:03,110 --> 00:10:07,190
Now, another example of
few-shot learning outside

214
00:10:07,190 --> 00:10:08,910
of reinforcement
learning is in-context

215
00:10:08,910 --> 00:10:11,110
learning in large
language models.

216
00:10:11,110 --> 00:10:13,770
And what this looks like
is, here's just one example.

217
00:10:13,770 --> 00:10:16,270
There's lots of examples of
different tasks you could do.

218
00:10:16,270 --> 00:10:20,910
In this case, the goal is, given
a sentence that's poor English

219
00:10:20,910 --> 00:10:23,430
input, like, "I eated
the purple berries,"

220
00:10:23,430 --> 00:10:27,110
output good English input like,
"I ate the purple berries."

221
00:10:27,110 --> 00:10:29,710
It gives it, in
this case, I think

222
00:10:29,710 --> 00:10:34,510
four or three different
examples of input-outputs.

223
00:10:34,510 --> 00:10:35,750
This is the training dataset.

224
00:10:35,750 --> 00:10:37,790
And the goal is for
a new input to be

225
00:10:37,790 --> 00:10:43,610
able to predict a good
output for that example.

226
00:10:43,610 --> 00:10:46,430
So here's another example of
a few-shot learning problem.

227
00:10:46,430 --> 00:10:48,770
And again, language
models get things that

228
00:10:48,770 --> 00:10:50,152
look like this during training.

229
00:10:50,152 --> 00:10:52,610
One thing that's a little bit
special about language models

230
00:10:52,610 --> 00:10:55,250
is that this sort of
in-context learning,

231
00:10:55,250 --> 00:10:58,050
you don't explicitly set up
your training dataset exactly

232
00:10:58,050 --> 00:10:59,450
to match all of this.

233
00:10:59,450 --> 00:11:02,010
And it turns out that a lot
of the statistics of data

234
00:11:02,010 --> 00:11:05,330
on the internet
leads to this ability

235
00:11:05,330 --> 00:11:09,012
to react to these few-shot
examples effectively.

236
00:11:09,012 --> 00:11:10,470
This is actually
just a base model.

237
00:11:10,470 --> 00:11:12,978
I think this is from GPT-3.

238
00:11:12,978 --> 00:11:15,270
And so even without any
additional supervised training,

239
00:11:15,270 --> 00:11:17,050
you get something like this.

240
00:11:17,050 --> 00:11:18,150
It's not perfect.

241
00:11:18,150 --> 00:11:19,610
It doesn't work all the time.

242
00:11:19,610 --> 00:11:23,792
But this is another example
of few-shot learning.

243
00:11:23,792 --> 00:11:25,250
And then in each
of these examples,

244
00:11:25,250 --> 00:11:26,970
you can think of
the goal as trying

245
00:11:26,970 --> 00:11:30,250
to train this model that
takes as input a training

246
00:11:30,250 --> 00:11:34,570
dataset and a new
example x, and makes

247
00:11:34,570 --> 00:11:37,970
a prediction for that
example, based on both x

248
00:11:37,970 --> 00:11:40,250
and on the training data.

249
00:11:40,250 --> 00:11:43,600
Yeah, so the question is, in
in-context learning in LLMs,

250
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:46,200
typically you achieve
this sort of model,

251
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:48,320
this few-shot learning
through prompting,

252
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:51,720
by putting the
dataset in a context

253
00:11:51,720 --> 00:11:54,200
or as part of the
prompt into the model.

254
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:56,132
Is that also how it
works in meta-RL?

255
00:11:56,132 --> 00:11:57,840
Generally, yes, we're
going to be looking

256
00:11:57,840 --> 00:11:59,280
at that case in meta-RL.

257
00:11:59,280 --> 00:12:02,640
There are actually other methods
where you can accomplish this,

258
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:04,960
actually, with gradient
updates as well,

259
00:12:04,960 --> 00:12:08,600
or with something that looks
like nearest neighbors.

260
00:12:08,600 --> 00:12:12,380
The most successful method in
meta-RL looks like prompting.

261
00:12:12,380 --> 00:12:14,830
And so that's what we'll
be talking about today.

262
00:12:14,830 --> 00:12:17,360

263
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:19,920
Now, let's look at
a meta-RL example.

264
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:26,680
So, say that we want
to navigate mazes.

265
00:12:26,680 --> 00:12:31,120
And so our goal is, we
want to solve a new maze.

266
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:34,340
And we're an agent
that's in this maze.

267
00:12:34,340 --> 00:12:35,423
We don't get to see.

268
00:12:35,423 --> 00:12:37,340
We actually don't even
see this top-down view.

269
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:40,080
Maybe we just have a
first-person perspective.

270
00:12:40,080 --> 00:12:42,710
And our goal is, because this
is a reinforcement learning

271
00:12:42,710 --> 00:12:45,033
problem, it's not just
about outputting a label.

272
00:12:45,033 --> 00:12:46,950
It's actually also about
collecting experience

273
00:12:46,950 --> 00:12:48,630
in the environment.

274
00:12:48,630 --> 00:12:51,550
And so the goal
is, we'd like to be

275
00:12:51,550 --> 00:12:54,330
able to very quickly solve a
reinforcement learning problem.

276
00:12:54,330 --> 00:12:57,430
And we'll frame this as
collecting a very small training

277
00:12:57,430 --> 00:13:00,430
dataset of one episode
or a few episodes.

278
00:13:00,430 --> 00:13:02,130
In this case, it's
exploring the maze,

279
00:13:02,130 --> 00:13:05,230
trying to find
where the goal is.

280
00:13:05,230 --> 00:13:08,570
And it starts in the middle
right here, explores over here.

281
00:13:08,570 --> 00:13:11,590
And it eventually goes
to the goal, right here.

282
00:13:11,590 --> 00:13:14,590
And after we explore,
we'd like to be

283
00:13:14,590 --> 00:13:16,950
able to have a policy
that is able to solve

284
00:13:16,950 --> 00:13:20,550
the maze, in this case,
just with a single episode

285
00:13:20,550 --> 00:13:23,030
of experience.

286
00:13:23,030 --> 00:13:25,270
And again, if you were
to do this sort of thing

287
00:13:25,270 --> 00:13:27,550
from scratch that
tries to use just

288
00:13:27,550 --> 00:13:29,110
a single episode of
experience to get

289
00:13:29,110 --> 00:13:32,150
a policy that can
solve the task,

290
00:13:32,150 --> 00:13:34,230
this would be very, very hard.

291
00:13:34,230 --> 00:13:36,870
And instead, the way
that meta-RL frames

292
00:13:36,870 --> 00:13:40,610
this is by trying to
construct examples

293
00:13:40,610 --> 00:13:43,210
and trying to construct a
set of training environments

294
00:13:43,210 --> 00:13:47,170
that facilitates this
kind of fast adaptation.

295
00:13:47,170 --> 00:13:49,670
Now, in comparison to the
supervised learning scenarios,

296
00:13:49,670 --> 00:13:51,850
like I mentioned,
we're now going

297
00:13:51,850 --> 00:13:54,070
to be faced with this
exploration problem.

298
00:13:54,070 --> 00:13:56,610
We're not just given
a training dataset

299
00:13:56,610 --> 00:13:59,590
and then make a prediction
based on that training dataset.

300
00:13:59,590 --> 00:14:03,330
We're actually constructing
the training dataset ourselves

301
00:14:03,330 --> 00:14:05,370
through exploration.

302
00:14:05,370 --> 00:14:07,450
And we need to figure
out how best to trade-off

303
00:14:07,450 --> 00:14:11,890
between exploring in the
environment versus being

304
00:14:11,890 --> 00:14:14,570
able to then solve the task; and
how much time we should spend

305
00:14:14,570 --> 00:14:17,290
exploring versus
trying to exploit what

306
00:14:17,290 --> 00:14:20,250
we know about the environment.

307
00:14:20,250 --> 00:14:22,582
So this is our goal.

308
00:14:22,582 --> 00:14:24,790
This is what we'd like to
be able to do at test time.

309
00:14:24,790 --> 00:14:29,170
We'd like to be able to
quickly learn a new maze.

310
00:14:29,170 --> 00:14:34,370
Then, to set our agent up for
success at meta-training time,

311
00:14:34,370 --> 00:14:36,870
before we try to
adapt to a new maze,

312
00:14:36,870 --> 00:14:40,360
we'll try to learn how to
efficiently explore and solve

313
00:14:40,360 --> 00:14:42,560
many other MDPs
that are related.

314
00:14:42,560 --> 00:14:46,880
In this case, they can be other
mazes like this, and so forth.

315
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:48,960
And these are each of
the tasks that we'll

316
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:52,030
be training on in order
to adapt to a new maze.

317
00:14:52,030 --> 00:14:55,400

318
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:56,320
Cool.

319
00:14:56,320 --> 00:14:58,960
The other thing, one
key assumption here

320
00:14:58,960 --> 00:15:00,500
is that you need to have--

321
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:02,560
these new MDPs
can't be completely

322
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:05,203
different from the MDPs
that you saw before.

323
00:15:05,203 --> 00:15:07,120
And the way that we can
think about that is we

324
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:09,600
should have some sort of
distribution over tasks.

325
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:12,680
And these new MDPs that
we're trying to adapt to

326
00:15:12,680 --> 00:15:15,000
should be within the
distribution of tasks

327
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:16,930
that we saw during training.

328
00:15:16,930 --> 00:15:23,040

329
00:15:23,040 --> 00:15:26,600
Maze navigation is one
example of a meta-RL task.

330
00:15:26,600 --> 00:15:29,960
There's lots of other
scenarios as well.

331
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:35,160
So one useful scenario
that we've seen in robotics

332
00:15:35,160 --> 00:15:39,270
is being able to
adapt very quickly

333
00:15:39,270 --> 00:15:43,290
to different terrains, different
slopes for legged robot systems.

334
00:15:43,290 --> 00:15:47,110
This is actually a really
small legged robot,

335
00:15:47,110 --> 00:15:51,610
but it's also applicable to
larger legged robots as well.

336
00:15:51,610 --> 00:15:53,190
And sometimes you
might actually be

337
00:15:53,190 --> 00:15:55,190
training on different
environments in simulation

338
00:15:55,190 --> 00:15:56,710
and then adapting
in the real world

339
00:15:56,710 --> 00:16:00,165
as just another environment.

340
00:16:00,165 --> 00:16:01,790
You could also have
object manipulation

341
00:16:01,790 --> 00:16:05,208
as different tasks, as well,
in manipulation scenarios.

342
00:16:05,208 --> 00:16:07,750
And then there's also a number
of language modeling scenarios

343
00:16:07,750 --> 00:16:09,470
where this is quite applicable.

344
00:16:09,470 --> 00:16:11,950
One example where
meta-RL has been relevant

345
00:16:11,950 --> 00:16:15,430
is adapting to different users
with different preferences,

346
00:16:15,430 --> 00:16:18,390
where you give examples
of the kinds of responses

347
00:16:18,390 --> 00:16:20,270
that they want in context.

348
00:16:20,270 --> 00:16:24,023
And we'll also later see an
example in reasoning as well.

349
00:16:24,023 --> 00:16:26,190
Yeah, so the question is,
what is the key difference

350
00:16:26,190 --> 00:16:29,750
between this sort
of meta-RL approach

351
00:16:29,750 --> 00:16:31,630
and transfer learning
where you're training

352
00:16:31,630 --> 00:16:33,270
and then fine-tuning?

353
00:16:33,270 --> 00:16:37,210
And there's a couple
of ways to view it.

354
00:16:37,210 --> 00:16:40,030
One is that during
the training process,

355
00:16:40,030 --> 00:16:42,950
you're actually explicitly
optimizing for transferability.

356
00:16:42,950 --> 00:16:44,810
You're optimizing
for the ability

357
00:16:44,810 --> 00:16:47,050
to adapt with a small dataset.

358
00:16:47,050 --> 00:16:51,070
And as a result of that, you
can get much faster transfer.

359
00:16:51,070 --> 00:16:53,450
You can use a much
smaller dataset.

360
00:16:53,450 --> 00:16:55,970
And there are examples
of, if you use fine-tuning

361
00:16:55,970 --> 00:16:58,530
versus if you use few-shot
in-context learning

362
00:16:58,530 --> 00:17:00,372
or few-shot
adaptation, you can get

363
00:17:00,372 --> 00:17:01,580
much more efficient transfer.

364
00:17:01,580 --> 00:17:04,670

365
00:17:04,670 --> 00:17:06,650
So you get much
greater efficiency.

366
00:17:06,650 --> 00:17:10,250
And the way that you
get that is by providing

367
00:17:10,250 --> 00:17:16,910
the algorithm with a number
of different training tasks,

368
00:17:16,910 --> 00:17:21,490
rather than just one training
task that you're feeding off of.

369
00:17:21,490 --> 00:17:23,849
So slightly more
formally, we've talked

370
00:17:23,849 --> 00:17:25,970
about multi-task
learning, where you

371
00:17:25,970 --> 00:17:27,470
try to solve multiple
tasks at once.

372
00:17:27,470 --> 00:17:31,650
You might try to transfer
to a new task from that.

373
00:17:31,650 --> 00:17:34,258
But you're not optimizing
for transferability.

374
00:17:34,258 --> 00:17:35,800
With transfer
learning, you're trying

375
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:40,400
to solve some target task
after solving some source

376
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:43,320
task, typically by fine-tuning.

377
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:45,440
Whereas, in the
meta-learning problem,

378
00:17:45,440 --> 00:17:49,640
we have experience from
some training tasks

379
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:54,468
and we want to very
quickly solve a new task.

380
00:17:54,468 --> 00:17:56,260
And in the reinforcement
learning scenario,

381
00:17:56,260 --> 00:18:00,120
these tasks are going to be
MDPs, each with their own state

382
00:18:00,120 --> 00:18:03,480
space, action space,
dynamics, reward.

383
00:18:03,480 --> 00:18:07,740
And we see meta-learning
and supervised learning.

384
00:18:07,740 --> 00:18:11,160
And also there's a nice
analogy between the two,

385
00:18:11,160 --> 00:18:13,120
where the main
difference with meta-RL

386
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:14,860
is this exploration challenge.

387
00:18:14,860 --> 00:18:19,430

388
00:18:19,430 --> 00:18:22,080
And then diving
into some notation

389
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:28,160
here, we can view the
meta-RL problem as basically,

390
00:18:28,160 --> 00:18:31,350
with a small amount
of experience,

391
00:18:31,350 --> 00:18:35,270
be able to learn a policy
that can solve the task,

392
00:18:35,270 --> 00:18:38,890
and also to be able to collect
this experience itself.

393
00:18:38,890 --> 00:18:41,070
And so this training
data, there's

394
00:18:41,070 --> 00:18:43,470
two different variants
of the meta-RL problem.

395
00:18:43,470 --> 00:18:47,470
In the first case, this can
correspond to multiple rollouts

396
00:18:47,470 --> 00:18:50,430
from some exploration policy.

397
00:18:50,430 --> 00:18:52,830
And instead, you
could also consider

398
00:18:52,830 --> 00:18:56,670
this to be just actually a few
time steps from your exploration

399
00:18:56,670 --> 00:18:57,590
policy.

400
00:18:57,590 --> 00:19:00,453
There's a bit of a continuum
between these two scenarios.

401
00:19:00,453 --> 00:19:01,870
You might want to
be able to adapt

402
00:19:01,870 --> 00:19:04,870
both online with
a few time steps,

403
00:19:04,870 --> 00:19:08,350
and with more and more episodes,
with basically a growing

404
00:19:08,350 --> 00:19:12,710
dataset, but writing
it explicitly

405
00:19:12,710 --> 00:19:17,105
as two different things
here to make it clear

406
00:19:17,105 --> 00:19:19,230
that there are some problem
settings where you want

407
00:19:19,230 --> 00:19:21,645
to adapt really quickly
online within an episode,

408
00:19:21,645 --> 00:19:23,270
and other scenarios
where you're caring

409
00:19:23,270 --> 00:19:28,632
a lot more about multiple
trials of experience.

410
00:19:28,632 --> 00:19:30,090
The last thing that
I'll mention is

411
00:19:30,090 --> 00:19:32,930
I've been writing it as an
exploration policy and a task

412
00:19:32,930 --> 00:19:34,203
policy.

413
00:19:34,203 --> 00:19:36,370
There are some scenarios
where you might train these

414
00:19:36,370 --> 00:19:37,910
and these might be
separate networks.

415
00:19:37,910 --> 00:19:39,368
There's other
scenarios where these

416
00:19:39,368 --> 00:19:42,330
could share parameters and
basically be the same network.

417
00:19:42,330 --> 00:19:46,090
So they don't necessarily
need to be separated

418
00:19:46,090 --> 00:19:49,170
like they are on this slide.

419
00:19:49,170 --> 00:19:51,030
And then the last
thing is, insofar

420
00:19:51,030 --> 00:19:52,530
as we're learning
this policy that's

421
00:19:52,530 --> 00:19:55,290
conditioning on a
training dataset,

422
00:19:55,290 --> 00:19:58,970
you can view this as
a task identifier.

423
00:19:58,970 --> 00:20:01,170
And from that
standpoint, we're then

424
00:20:01,170 --> 00:20:05,050
more in a setting that looks
like multi-task learning.

425
00:20:05,050 --> 00:20:07,503
So the question is, when
I say that the test has

426
00:20:07,503 --> 00:20:09,670
to be in distribution
compared to the training task,

427
00:20:09,670 --> 00:20:10,810
does that mean it has
to be the same as one

428
00:20:10,810 --> 00:20:11,727
of the training tasks?

429
00:20:11,727 --> 00:20:13,490
Or can it be a bit different?

430
00:20:13,490 --> 00:20:16,105
It can be a bit different.

431
00:20:16,105 --> 00:20:20,890
And we'll see examples where
it is different as well.

432
00:20:20,890 --> 00:20:23,790
So let's talk about how
to address these problems.

433
00:20:23,790 --> 00:20:31,600
So the good news is that there's
a very conceptually simple way

434
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:35,960
to address this general
problem statement.

435
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:38,360
The bad news is that it can
be challenging to optimize,

436
00:20:38,360 --> 00:20:38,900
in practice.

437
00:20:38,900 --> 00:20:41,440
And we'll talk about some of
those challenges in a bit.

438
00:20:41,440 --> 00:20:45,760
But conceptually,
we can essentially

439
00:20:45,760 --> 00:20:49,840
train a sequence model.

440
00:20:49,840 --> 00:20:51,400
It could be a
transformer, any sort

441
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:54,720
of neural network with memory,
a recurrent neural network.

442
00:20:54,720 --> 00:20:59,680
And use this, where
basically everything that's

443
00:20:59,680 --> 00:21:03,000
being passed into this policy
corresponds to the experience

444
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,800
that the agent has collected.

445
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:08,280
And so you're basically
conditioning your policy

446
00:21:08,280 --> 00:21:10,360
on this training dataset.

447
00:21:10,360 --> 00:21:14,680
And then having a policy output
actions based off of the data

448
00:21:14,680 --> 00:21:17,880
that it's seen so
far in that MDP.

449
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:24,440
And then training the
sequence model policy

450
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:30,110
across all of the set of
training MDPs that we have.

451
00:21:30,110 --> 00:21:33,190
So here, this is
the current state.

452
00:21:33,190 --> 00:21:34,910
This sort of context
or training dataset

453
00:21:34,910 --> 00:21:37,630
is going to get larger
over time as we collect

454
00:21:37,630 --> 00:21:42,500
more experience in a given MDP.

455
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:45,870

456
00:21:45,870 --> 00:21:48,230
Now, a question for you.

457
00:21:48,230 --> 00:21:50,150
There are a couple
of differences

458
00:21:50,150 --> 00:21:53,870
between this and simply
using a recurrent policy

459
00:21:53,870 --> 00:21:57,470
in a standard RL setup--

460
00:21:57,470 --> 00:21:59,930
or using a sequence model
in a standard RL setup,

461
00:21:59,930 --> 00:22:01,430
like using a transformer.

462
00:22:01,430 --> 00:22:03,190
Does anyone have
thoughts on what

463
00:22:03,190 --> 00:22:08,310
are some of the
differences between what

464
00:22:08,310 --> 00:22:12,510
I'm starting to talk about here
and simply using a sequence

465
00:22:12,510 --> 00:22:16,910
model as your policy
in a standard RL setup?

466
00:22:16,910 --> 00:22:18,470
Memory.

467
00:22:18,470 --> 00:22:22,810
Yeah, so typically in RL,
we often don't use memory.

468
00:22:22,810 --> 00:22:24,430
And here we're using memory.

469
00:22:24,430 --> 00:22:26,250
So that's one thing.

470
00:22:26,250 --> 00:22:31,170
But compared to if we had used
memory in a standard RL setup,

471
00:22:31,170 --> 00:22:33,143
what else is different here?

472
00:22:33,143 --> 00:22:34,810
Yeah, so one thing
that we're doing here

473
00:22:34,810 --> 00:22:37,830
is we're actually passing the
reward as input into the memory.

474
00:22:37,830 --> 00:22:41,410
And that's a little bit
different in the sense

475
00:22:41,410 --> 00:22:43,810
that you're actually
using reward information

476
00:22:43,810 --> 00:22:48,810
from the current MDP in order to
try to get better at the task.

477
00:22:48,810 --> 00:22:50,770
This also means that
actually at test time

478
00:22:50,770 --> 00:22:52,950
or when we're trying
to adapt to a new task,

479
00:22:52,950 --> 00:22:55,170
we're also going to
be using the reward.

480
00:22:55,170 --> 00:22:56,890
And in RL settings,
oftentimes we

481
00:22:56,890 --> 00:22:59,930
don't necessarily assume that we
have the reward when we're just

482
00:22:59,930 --> 00:23:02,710
deploying our policy.

483
00:23:02,710 --> 00:23:05,810
So that was one main difference.

484
00:23:05,810 --> 00:23:09,170
Any other differences?

485
00:23:09,170 --> 00:23:11,410
Yeah, so the question is,
why are we also not passing

486
00:23:11,410 --> 00:23:12,570
as input the actions?

487
00:23:12,570 --> 00:23:15,410
You can pass as
input the actions.

488
00:23:15,410 --> 00:23:20,750
It depends on, if your policy
is mostly deterministic,

489
00:23:20,750 --> 00:23:22,530
then information
about past actions

490
00:23:22,530 --> 00:23:24,765
could be stored in
the memory as well.

491
00:23:24,765 --> 00:23:27,140
Because you were predicting,
you generated those actions.

492
00:23:27,140 --> 00:23:29,223
And so in those cases, you
don't need the actions.

493
00:23:29,223 --> 00:23:31,120
If you have a more
stochastic policy,

494
00:23:31,120 --> 00:23:34,743
then that sampling process won't
be necessarily in the memory.

495
00:23:34,743 --> 00:23:37,160
And in those cases, it's good
to pass as input the actions

496
00:23:37,160 --> 00:23:38,760
as well.

497
00:23:38,760 --> 00:23:42,920
Right, so the note was that the
states and actions might not be

498
00:23:42,920 --> 00:23:44,260
the same for different tasks.

499
00:23:44,260 --> 00:23:45,968
And so one of the
things that's different

500
00:23:45,968 --> 00:23:49,317
here is we're actually training
this model across MDPs.

501
00:23:49,317 --> 00:23:51,400
And so you might have a
different state and action

502
00:23:51,400 --> 00:23:51,700
space.

503
00:23:51,700 --> 00:23:53,180
You might have
different dynamics.

504
00:23:53,180 --> 00:23:55,960
And so one of the big
differences compared

505
00:23:55,960 --> 00:23:59,060
to standard RL is that actually
we're training this across MDPs.

506
00:23:59,060 --> 00:24:01,400
And there's going to be
differences in states, actions,

507
00:24:01,400 --> 00:24:05,780
dynamics, and rewards
across those MDPs.

508
00:24:05,780 --> 00:24:06,280
Cool.

509
00:24:06,280 --> 00:24:08,240
There's actually also
one other difference.

510
00:24:08,240 --> 00:24:09,907
But the first thing
that we talked about

511
00:24:09,907 --> 00:24:12,020
is, we see that the
reward is passed as input.

512
00:24:12,020 --> 00:24:14,280
You might also be
passing as input actions.

513
00:24:14,280 --> 00:24:17,000
And this is trained
across multiple MDPs.

514
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:20,720
And that's quite different
from the typical reinforcement

515
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:22,470
learning setup.

516
00:24:22,470 --> 00:24:24,630
Now, the second thing
that's also different

517
00:24:24,630 --> 00:24:28,150
is that your memory
is also going

518
00:24:28,150 --> 00:24:31,250
to span across multiple
episodes, potentially.

519
00:24:31,250 --> 00:24:34,030
If you're trying
to actually learn

520
00:24:34,030 --> 00:24:38,030
not just within an episode,
but learn across episodes, then

521
00:24:38,030 --> 00:24:40,870
rather than having your current
policy simply have memory

522
00:24:40,870 --> 00:24:43,790
within an episode, you
will be maintaining

523
00:24:43,790 --> 00:24:46,590
any sort of hidden state
or any sort of memory,

524
00:24:46,590 --> 00:24:49,530
not just within your episode,
but across multiple episodes.

525
00:24:49,530 --> 00:24:52,130

526
00:24:52,130 --> 00:24:54,890
Maybe I'll leave this up
here in case it's useful.

527
00:24:54,890 --> 00:24:58,270
In terms of the algorithm,
what this looks like is,

528
00:24:58,270 --> 00:25:06,230
we will sample a task or an MDP.

529
00:25:06,230 --> 00:25:13,430
So maybe let's call this MDP Ti.

530
00:25:13,430 --> 00:25:18,310
And then we're going to
run our policy in that MDP

531
00:25:18,310 --> 00:25:21,290
for multiple episodes.

532
00:25:21,290 --> 00:25:34,050
So let's say, run pi in our
task or MDP for n episodes.

533
00:25:34,050 --> 00:25:36,290
So this is rolling
out this right here.

534
00:25:36,290 --> 00:25:41,410
And it's going to have memory
across those N episodes.

535
00:25:41,410 --> 00:25:45,690
And then, optionally, if we're
using an off-policy algorithm

536
00:25:45,690 --> 00:25:51,130
with a replay buffer, then
we would store the episodes

537
00:25:51,130 --> 00:25:53,220
in a replay buffer.

538
00:25:53,220 --> 00:25:59,290

539
00:25:59,290 --> 00:26:04,210
And then lastly, we're
going to update our policy

540
00:26:04,210 --> 00:26:07,890
to optimize the rewards.

541
00:26:07,890 --> 00:26:12,930
And so we will be updating pi.

542
00:26:12,930 --> 00:26:17,850
And specifically, we are
going to be maximizing

543
00:26:17,850 --> 00:26:20,680
the sum of rewards, like
the typical RL objective,

544
00:26:20,680 --> 00:26:24,920
but it's going to be across
all of the tasks in our task

545
00:26:24,920 --> 00:26:25,700
distribution.

546
00:26:25,700 --> 00:26:34,080
So we'll have some sum over
tasks i, in some mini-batch,

547
00:26:34,080 --> 00:26:39,800
and then also the
expected sum of rewards.

548
00:26:39,800 --> 00:26:44,000
So this would be an
expectation under our policy

549
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:48,870
of the sum of rewards
over different time steps.

550
00:26:48,870 --> 00:26:55,560

551
00:26:55,560 --> 00:27:01,280
Now, this is a pretty
simple algorithm.

552
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:03,480
It looks a lot like
reinforcement learning,

553
00:27:03,480 --> 00:27:05,520
where simply the main
thing that's different

554
00:27:05,520 --> 00:27:07,600
is that we're going to be
doing this optimization

555
00:27:07,600 --> 00:27:09,320
across multiple MDPs.

556
00:27:09,320 --> 00:27:14,600
And our policy will have memory
across a set of episodes.

557
00:27:14,600 --> 00:27:19,150
Usually N, usually this is
going to be pretty small.

558
00:27:19,150 --> 00:27:22,530
Because if it was large, if
it was like 1,000 episodes,

559
00:27:22,530 --> 00:27:27,050
it's going to be hard to store
that in the context of a model,

560
00:27:27,050 --> 00:27:32,190
just because your memory will
start to increase substantially.

561
00:27:32,190 --> 00:27:37,270
So what that looks like is
you have this neural network.

562
00:27:37,270 --> 00:27:40,550
It has multiple episodes.

563
00:27:40,550 --> 00:27:45,170
And then you'll be rolling
it out for multiple episodes,

564
00:27:45,170 --> 00:27:48,450
taking actions, and then you'll
start collecting for a new MDP.

565
00:27:48,450 --> 00:27:50,950
So you'll cut your
memory when you

566
00:27:50,950 --> 00:27:54,190
start to collect for the
new MDP that you've sampled.

567
00:27:54,190 --> 00:27:57,170
So you sample a task, roll
out the policy for n episodes.

568
00:27:57,170 --> 00:28:00,710
In this case, n equals
2 in this diagram,

569
00:28:00,710 --> 00:28:04,590
under the dynamics and
reward function for that MDP.

570
00:28:04,590 --> 00:28:06,450
Then, store in
your replay buffer.

571
00:28:06,450 --> 00:28:09,790
Oftentimes, you'll have a
replay buffer for each task.

572
00:28:09,790 --> 00:28:13,390
And then update your policy to
maximize the discounted return

573
00:28:13,390 --> 00:28:15,690
for all tasks that
you've collected for.

574
00:28:15,690 --> 00:28:17,930
And then repeat this
process in order

575
00:28:17,930 --> 00:28:19,690
to continuously
get a policy that's

576
00:28:19,690 --> 00:28:24,530
better at doing this
in-context adaptation.

577
00:28:24,530 --> 00:28:25,910
Yeah, so that's a good question.

578
00:28:25,910 --> 00:28:27,810
So the question is,
does it know that it's

579
00:28:27,810 --> 00:28:31,290
starting a new episode, like
at this position right here?

580
00:28:31,290 --> 00:28:32,930
You could use a
position encoding.

581
00:28:32,930 --> 00:28:36,450
In practice, you're also
going to probably have

582
00:28:36,450 --> 00:28:38,070
a discontinuity in your state.

583
00:28:38,070 --> 00:28:39,570
And so you might
just be able to see

584
00:28:39,570 --> 00:28:42,610
that your current state is very
different from the past state.

585
00:28:42,610 --> 00:28:44,070
And that might also tell it.

586
00:28:44,070 --> 00:28:45,862
But yeah, you could
have some sort of token

587
00:28:45,862 --> 00:28:48,170
to also tell it that
it's a new episode.

588
00:28:48,170 --> 00:28:50,290
The question is, are
we randomizing S1

589
00:28:50,290 --> 00:28:52,130
across the different episodes?

590
00:28:52,130 --> 00:28:55,410
When you're rolling
out the policy, when

591
00:28:55,410 --> 00:28:57,730
you start a new
episode, that will

592
00:28:57,730 --> 00:29:00,810
be a different sample from the
initial state distribution.

593
00:29:00,810 --> 00:29:03,870
And so, S1, I guess it's
maybe a little confusing.

594
00:29:03,870 --> 00:29:05,970
S1 here might be
different from S1 here.

595
00:29:05,970 --> 00:29:10,010
S2 is very likely different
from S2 here, and so forth.

596
00:29:10,010 --> 00:29:10,590
Cool.

597
00:29:10,590 --> 00:29:13,010
And then the outcome
of this is you'll

598
00:29:13,010 --> 00:29:18,280
have a policy that should be
able to adapt across episodes

599
00:29:18,280 --> 00:29:20,600
with experience
from those episodes.

600
00:29:20,600 --> 00:29:24,140
And it should be
able to, on its own,

601
00:29:24,140 --> 00:29:27,180
actually decide on the best
exploration/exploitation

602
00:29:27,180 --> 00:29:28,200
trade-off.

603
00:29:28,200 --> 00:29:30,100
And the best
exploration/exploitation

604
00:29:30,100 --> 00:29:33,360
trade-off is going to depend
on the number of episodes

605
00:29:33,360 --> 00:29:34,720
that you give it.

606
00:29:34,720 --> 00:29:37,840
If N is 2, then it
has two episodes

607
00:29:37,840 --> 00:29:40,920
to maximize rewards as
quickly as possible.

608
00:29:40,920 --> 00:29:42,620
And if and if it
needs to explore,

609
00:29:42,620 --> 00:29:44,460
it'll explore as
quickly as possible.

610
00:29:44,460 --> 00:29:46,320
If N is a little bit
larger, then you've

611
00:29:46,320 --> 00:29:48,720
given it a little
bit more budget--

612
00:29:48,720 --> 00:29:51,920
it still wants to explore
quickly and efficiently,

613
00:29:51,920 --> 00:29:54,540
if it can, because then it
can get higher rewards later.

614
00:29:54,540 --> 00:29:57,720
But it might have slightly
more budget to explore.

615
00:29:57,720 --> 00:30:01,120
And so one of the things that's
pretty cool about this, when you

616
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:03,020
optimize this whole
thing end-to-end,

617
00:30:03,020 --> 00:30:06,560
is that it will make
decisions on whether or not

618
00:30:06,560 --> 00:30:09,680
to explore or to exploit.

619
00:30:09,680 --> 00:30:11,320
And it'll make those
decisions in a way

620
00:30:11,320 --> 00:30:16,350
that's informed by whatever
gets it the highest reward.

621
00:30:16,350 --> 00:30:18,730
Then, after this
training process,

622
00:30:18,730 --> 00:30:24,810
after you've trained
it on multiple MDPs,

623
00:30:24,810 --> 00:30:29,310
you can actually then roll
out your policy in a new task.

624
00:30:29,310 --> 00:30:39,170
So at test time, you will
then give it a new task.

625
00:30:39,170 --> 00:30:48,030
So you're in some new MDP, Tj.

626
00:30:48,030 --> 00:30:53,270
And you can take the
policy that was trained

627
00:30:53,270 --> 00:30:58,570
and roll it out in that MDP.

628
00:30:58,570 --> 00:31:07,310
So roll out pi in that MDP
with those states, actions,

629
00:31:07,310 --> 00:31:09,110
and rewards, and so forth.

630
00:31:09,110 --> 00:31:11,970
And so sample new task,
roll out that policy.

631
00:31:11,970 --> 00:31:15,250
You want to roll this
out for up to N episodes.

632
00:31:15,250 --> 00:31:17,890
If you roll it out
for longer, all bets

633
00:31:17,890 --> 00:31:19,630
are off, in terms
of what it might do.

634
00:31:19,630 --> 00:31:23,568

635
00:31:23,568 --> 00:31:25,610
You could also have a
variable number of episodes

636
00:31:25,610 --> 00:31:27,490
that you use during
training, if you

637
00:31:27,490 --> 00:31:30,930
want it to be able to handle
variable numbers of episodes.

638
00:31:30,930 --> 00:31:36,290
But yeah, this would be like
for N episodes or for up to N

639
00:31:36,290 --> 00:31:37,110
episodes.

640
00:31:37,110 --> 00:31:43,250

641
00:31:43,250 --> 00:31:44,790
Cool.

642
00:31:44,790 --> 00:31:46,708
So in terms of actually
implementing this,

643
00:31:46,708 --> 00:31:48,250
there's a few
different architectures

644
00:31:48,250 --> 00:31:49,870
and optimizers you could use.

645
00:31:49,870 --> 00:31:53,170
So you could use a vanilla
recurrent neural network.

646
00:31:53,170 --> 00:31:57,170
Some of the first works on meta
reinforcement learning did this.

647
00:31:57,170 --> 00:31:59,070
And they used algorithms.

648
00:31:59,070 --> 00:32:02,590
This is, I think, before PPO
existed or was introduced.

649
00:32:02,590 --> 00:32:04,210
They used an algorithm
that's somewhat

650
00:32:04,210 --> 00:32:08,610
similar in nature, in terms
of the assumptions, as PPO.

651
00:32:08,610 --> 00:32:12,760
There's is also a paper that
before transformers were really

652
00:32:12,760 --> 00:32:17,220
popular, used self-attention
and also 1D convolutions.

653
00:32:17,220 --> 00:32:19,360
You can think of it
sort of a transformer.

654
00:32:19,360 --> 00:32:20,648
It optimized this.

655
00:32:20,648 --> 00:32:22,440
And you can actually
note that they do also

656
00:32:22,440 --> 00:32:28,320
pass the actions as input
in this particular scenario.

657
00:32:28,320 --> 00:32:33,180
One other thing, we'll talk
about this a bit more later.

658
00:32:33,180 --> 00:32:37,200
But you can also, instead of
using a transformer or an RNN,

659
00:32:37,200 --> 00:32:43,120
you could also use a feedforward
network and then average across,

660
00:32:43,120 --> 00:32:44,520
average the embedding.

661
00:32:44,520 --> 00:32:47,840
This is sometimes
called a deep set.

662
00:32:47,840 --> 00:32:50,580
And it has its own benefits.

663
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:52,560
It's actually pretty
computationally fast,

664
00:32:52,560 --> 00:32:54,498
because you can
parallelize everything.

665
00:32:54,498 --> 00:32:56,040
And also, actually,
it's surprisingly

666
00:32:56,040 --> 00:32:58,928
expressive for what it can do.

667
00:32:58,928 --> 00:33:00,220
So let's look at some examples.

668
00:33:00,220 --> 00:33:04,120
So we'll look at this example
that was using something that's

669
00:33:04,120 --> 00:33:06,080
a bit like a transformer.

670
00:33:06,080 --> 00:33:08,230
And they were
trying to learn how

671
00:33:08,230 --> 00:33:10,790
to visually navigate a maze--

672
00:33:10,790 --> 00:33:14,870
specifically, new mazes that the
agent had never been in before.

673
00:33:14,870 --> 00:33:18,350
They trained it on
1,000 small mazes.

674
00:33:18,350 --> 00:33:21,310
And they tested it
on held-out mazes

675
00:33:21,310 --> 00:33:23,150
that the agent
hadn't seen before.

676
00:33:23,150 --> 00:33:25,190
And they tested it actually
both on small mazes

677
00:33:25,190 --> 00:33:26,610
and on large mazes.

678
00:33:26,610 --> 00:33:28,110
So this is actually
a scenario where

679
00:33:28,110 --> 00:33:30,855
they were able to see some
extrapolation to tasks

680
00:33:30,855 --> 00:33:32,730
that were outside of
the distribution of what

681
00:33:32,730 --> 00:33:35,000
it was trained on.

682
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:36,250
So this is what it looks like.

683
00:33:36,250 --> 00:33:37,765
So this is at test time.

684
00:33:37,765 --> 00:33:39,390
And you can see that
the agent, it just

685
00:33:39,390 --> 00:33:42,030
is exploring around
this environment,

686
00:33:42,030 --> 00:33:44,050
looking for where the goal is.

687
00:33:44,050 --> 00:33:46,550
It only gets these
images as input.

688
00:33:46,550 --> 00:33:49,930
And on the right, we can see
the trace of the exploration.

689
00:33:49,930 --> 00:33:52,310
So it started in the
middle, explored over here,

690
00:33:52,310 --> 00:33:55,510
it explored down here, and
eventually found the goal.

691
00:33:55,510 --> 00:33:57,640
And then after that
exploration episode,

692
00:33:57,640 --> 00:34:00,390
we still have memory from
that previous episode.

693
00:34:00,390 --> 00:34:05,270
And using that memory,
it's able to then

694
00:34:05,270 --> 00:34:10,530
be able to go
directly to the goal,

695
00:34:10,530 --> 00:34:14,170
based on this
experience in that maze.

696
00:34:14,170 --> 00:34:16,370
So watching that again, whoops.

697
00:34:16,370 --> 00:34:19,130

698
00:34:19,130 --> 00:34:21,030
This is the first episode.

699
00:34:21,030 --> 00:34:26,570
And so this is the first episode
of rolling out this policy.

700
00:34:26,570 --> 00:34:30,022
It's choosing to explore
because it doesn't know

701
00:34:30,022 --> 00:34:31,550
where the goal is in this maze.

702
00:34:31,550 --> 00:34:33,850
It also doesn't know
the layout of the maze.

703
00:34:33,850 --> 00:34:36,210
And so we see it explore here.

704
00:34:36,210 --> 00:34:39,210
And then in the
second episode, it

705
00:34:39,210 --> 00:34:40,929
chooses to go
straight to the goal

706
00:34:40,929 --> 00:34:43,570
because it decided that
it already explored

707
00:34:43,570 --> 00:34:45,650
the extent of the maze.

708
00:34:45,650 --> 00:34:47,350
Here's an example
in a larger maze.

709
00:34:47,350 --> 00:34:50,370
So the agent starts over here.

710
00:34:50,370 --> 00:34:58,730
And it explores in the top
part, doesn't see the goal.

711
00:34:58,730 --> 00:35:00,807
It realizes that
this is a dead end.

712
00:35:00,807 --> 00:35:02,390
It doesn't need to
explore it further.

713
00:35:02,390 --> 00:35:04,280
And then it ends up
seeing the goal here.

714
00:35:04,280 --> 00:35:09,160
And then after that, it
goes straight to the goal

715
00:35:09,160 --> 00:35:10,760
after that first episode.

716
00:35:10,760 --> 00:35:15,240
And so in this training
example, N equals 2.

717
00:35:15,240 --> 00:35:19,320
So they trained it just to be
able to explore and exploit

718
00:35:19,320 --> 00:35:21,520
after two episodes.

719
00:35:21,520 --> 00:35:22,040
Cool.

720
00:35:22,040 --> 00:35:24,640
And then quantitatively, they
looked at the average time

721
00:35:24,640 --> 00:35:27,200
to find the goal in
both the first episode

722
00:35:27,200 --> 00:35:29,000
and the second episode.

723
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:34,360
They compared to using a random
policy, which occasionally does

724
00:35:34,360 --> 00:35:37,320
happen upon the goal,
and using an LSTM

725
00:35:37,320 --> 00:35:40,160
architecture and this more
self-attention and convolution

726
00:35:40,160 --> 00:35:41,200
architecture.

727
00:35:41,200 --> 00:35:44,262
And they found that with
the small mazes, even

728
00:35:44,262 --> 00:35:46,720
within the first episode, they
were able to do a lot better

729
00:35:46,720 --> 00:35:47,720
than random.

730
00:35:47,720 --> 00:35:50,440
That's because it's actually
exploring efficiently and then

731
00:35:50,440 --> 00:35:52,560
eventually often
finding the goal.

732
00:35:52,560 --> 00:35:56,740
And then it's able to
find the goal faster

733
00:35:56,740 --> 00:35:58,640
in the second episode,
after learning

734
00:35:58,640 --> 00:36:01,520
where the goal is and about
the maze in the first episode.

735
00:36:01,520 --> 00:36:03,740
We also see that it does
significantly better

736
00:36:03,740 --> 00:36:06,180
than a random agent
in the large mazes.

737
00:36:06,180 --> 00:36:09,860
And in both cases, using the
self-attention architecture

738
00:36:09,860 --> 00:36:13,100
is better than using an
LSTM architecture, which

739
00:36:13,100 --> 00:36:15,385
is another memory architecture.

740
00:36:15,385 --> 00:36:17,760
Yeah, so the question is, is
the reward to find the goal?

741
00:36:17,760 --> 00:36:21,940
And if so, why is it optimized
to find it as fast as possible?

742
00:36:21,940 --> 00:36:27,100
So the reward is
to find the goal.

743
00:36:27,100 --> 00:36:30,860
I don't remember the exact
structure of the reward.

744
00:36:30,860 --> 00:36:33,800
It's optimized to
find it quickly.

745
00:36:33,800 --> 00:36:35,700
There's a couple of
ways that lead to it

746
00:36:35,700 --> 00:36:39,060
to optimize to find it quickly.

747
00:36:39,060 --> 00:36:41,620
In this particular
case, I believe they're

748
00:36:41,620 --> 00:36:43,040
using discounted rewards.

749
00:36:43,040 --> 00:36:46,530
And so the discount factor,
instead of being like this,

750
00:36:46,530 --> 00:36:48,860
you're optimizing for
discounted rewards.

751
00:36:48,860 --> 00:36:50,820
So there's a discount
factor right there.

752
00:36:50,820 --> 00:36:52,280
And the discount
factor is not 1.

753
00:36:52,280 --> 00:36:55,620
It's probably like 0.95 or 0.99.

754
00:36:55,620 --> 00:36:59,100
And so that objective
will be higher

755
00:36:59,100 --> 00:37:01,970
if you get the reward
sooner rather than later.

756
00:37:01,970 --> 00:37:05,110
Yeah, so the question is, what
is passed as input to the model?

757
00:37:05,110 --> 00:37:08,550
Yeah, exactly, so it's video
from the previous episode,

758
00:37:08,550 --> 00:37:11,262
the actions from the
previous episode.

759
00:37:11,262 --> 00:37:13,470
Basically, everything up
until the current time step,

760
00:37:13,470 --> 00:37:16,730
which is any previous
episodes and everything

761
00:37:16,730 --> 00:37:17,830
so far in that episode.

762
00:37:17,830 --> 00:37:21,610
So it's all of the video frames
and then also all of the actions

763
00:37:21,610 --> 00:37:24,810
and all of the rewards as well.

764
00:37:24,810 --> 00:37:26,930
Yeah, so the question is,
where in the algorithm

765
00:37:26,930 --> 00:37:29,770
are we exploring exploration
versus exploitation?

766
00:37:29,770 --> 00:37:35,530
And, here, it basically
all comes down

767
00:37:35,530 --> 00:37:37,530
to this objective
for both of them.

768
00:37:37,530 --> 00:37:46,010
And the insight is that
the agent doesn't know much

769
00:37:46,010 --> 00:37:46,710
about the task.

770
00:37:46,710 --> 00:37:47,690
It doesn't know.

771
00:37:47,690 --> 00:37:51,150
Well, depending on the
training tasks, in this case,

772
00:37:51,150 --> 00:37:52,910
it doesn't know
what maze it's in.

773
00:37:52,910 --> 00:37:55,618
It doesn't know the structure of
the maze and that sort of thing.

774
00:37:55,618 --> 00:37:58,850
And so actually it's not
able to get high reward

775
00:37:58,850 --> 00:38:01,880
until it starts to
explore, until it finds

776
00:38:01,880 --> 00:38:04,080
where the goal is, for example.

777
00:38:04,080 --> 00:38:08,100
And so by optimizing just
end-to-end for reward,

778
00:38:08,100 --> 00:38:11,030
you're incentivizing it to--

779
00:38:11,030 --> 00:38:13,280
it's not going to get high
reward without exploration,

780
00:38:13,280 --> 00:38:14,800
essentially.

781
00:38:14,800 --> 00:38:18,093
Yeah, we will talk about
that soon in a few lectures.

782
00:38:18,093 --> 00:38:19,760
But yeah, there's
lots of problems here.

783
00:38:19,760 --> 00:38:21,720
Because when your policy
is bad and you're not

784
00:38:21,720 --> 00:38:25,760
getting good rewards, it ends up
being very difficult to figure

785
00:38:25,760 --> 00:38:28,160
out a good policy.

786
00:38:28,160 --> 00:38:33,200
Yeah, so the question
is, instead of

787
00:38:33,200 --> 00:38:35,698
doing what we're doing,
could we just do regular RL

788
00:38:35,698 --> 00:38:36,990
and then do test-time training?

789
00:38:36,990 --> 00:38:39,720

790
00:38:39,720 --> 00:38:46,082
So in some ways, language
models with recurrence,

791
00:38:46,082 --> 00:38:48,040
it depends on how you
view it, but in some ways

792
00:38:48,040 --> 00:38:50,123
you are already doing
something that kind of looks

793
00:38:50,123 --> 00:38:51,900
like meta-RL, if it has memory.

794
00:38:51,900 --> 00:38:53,640
It basically has the
ability to adapt,

795
00:38:53,640 --> 00:38:58,300
depending on the task
that you're giving it.

796
00:38:58,300 --> 00:39:00,103
And then one important
component is,

797
00:39:00,103 --> 00:39:01,520
if you're doing
test-time compute,

798
00:39:01,520 --> 00:39:05,140
you're allowing it to actually
think through the problem.

799
00:39:05,140 --> 00:39:07,340
Now, test-time compute
is a very broad term.

800
00:39:07,340 --> 00:39:12,460
So on the next slide, we'll
talk about how meta-RL is

801
00:39:12,460 --> 00:39:13,800
relevant for test-time compute.

802
00:39:13,800 --> 00:39:15,580
So let's talk about that now.

803
00:39:15,580 --> 00:39:18,640
So say that we're solving
different math problems.

804
00:39:18,640 --> 00:39:21,380
This is the canonical language
model example, especially

805
00:39:21,380 --> 00:39:23,460
for test-time compute.

806
00:39:23,460 --> 00:39:25,180
And one example
of a math problem

807
00:39:25,180 --> 00:39:27,060
is trying to show
this inequality.

808
00:39:27,060 --> 00:39:29,660
This is actually the same, I
think, possibly the same math

809
00:39:29,660 --> 00:39:32,700
problem that [INAUDIBLE]
showed in his lecture.

810
00:39:32,700 --> 00:39:37,940
And in some ways, trying
to solve this math problem,

811
00:39:37,940 --> 00:39:40,140
you can think of it
in some ways as an MDP

812
00:39:40,140 --> 00:39:43,940
itself, in that there's
different strategies that you

813
00:39:43,940 --> 00:39:47,100
might try to take to
solve this math problem.

814
00:39:47,100 --> 00:39:50,458
And you don't know, a priori,
which strategy is going

815
00:39:50,458 --> 00:39:51,750
to help you solve that problem.

816
00:39:51,750 --> 00:39:55,340

817
00:39:55,340 --> 00:39:56,305
Where do you start?

818
00:39:56,305 --> 00:39:58,430
Do you try to, for example,
do you guess and check?

819
00:39:58,430 --> 00:40:04,090
Do you try to eliminate terms
in the equation and so forth.

820
00:40:04,090 --> 00:40:06,770
And ideally, you'd
be able to explore

821
00:40:06,770 --> 00:40:10,450
those different strategies and
then produce an answer based off

822
00:40:10,450 --> 00:40:11,620
of the best strategy.

823
00:40:11,620 --> 00:40:17,410

824
00:40:17,410 --> 00:40:19,570
So DeepSeekR1 is
a language model

825
00:40:19,570 --> 00:40:23,150
that uses test-time compute in
order to solve math problems.

826
00:40:23,150 --> 00:40:27,730
And if you give R1 different
math problems to solve,

827
00:40:27,730 --> 00:40:30,490
if you give it a hard problem,
it thinks for a while.

828
00:40:30,490 --> 00:40:33,587
At some point, it cuts
off its test-time compute

829
00:40:33,587 --> 00:40:35,170
because it doesn't
want to be thinking

830
00:40:35,170 --> 00:40:36,730
for too long for the user.

831
00:40:36,730 --> 00:40:41,730
And then it outputs some
explanation for the user.

832
00:40:41,730 --> 00:40:44,450
And then also, if you give
it a really easy problem,

833
00:40:44,450 --> 00:40:46,650
like 2 plus 2 equals
question mark,

834
00:40:46,650 --> 00:40:50,450
it actually also kind of thinks
through what the solution might

835
00:40:50,450 --> 00:40:54,690
be and then gives a step-by-step
explanation for the answer.

836
00:40:54,690 --> 00:40:57,960
And so this is something that
is clearly somewhat wasteful

837
00:40:57,960 --> 00:41:02,800
to do, probably, unless this is
somehow a challenging problem.

838
00:41:02,800 --> 00:41:06,480
And so you can actually
use this notion of meta-RL

839
00:41:06,480 --> 00:41:08,900
to try to optimize
for test-time compute.

840
00:41:08,900 --> 00:41:10,348
And if you know
that you're going

841
00:41:10,348 --> 00:41:12,140
to be doing test-time
compute at test time,

842
00:41:12,140 --> 00:41:14,760
then basically bring that
into the training process

843
00:41:14,760 --> 00:41:17,862
and encourage the model
to efficiently use

844
00:41:17,862 --> 00:41:19,320
that test-time
compute, efficiently

845
00:41:19,320 --> 00:41:21,440
explore different
strategies, such that,

846
00:41:21,440 --> 00:41:24,920
ideally, on easy
problems, it is trying

847
00:41:24,920 --> 00:41:27,000
to output an answer as
quickly as possible.

848
00:41:27,000 --> 00:41:29,800
And for harder
problems, it's thinking

849
00:41:29,800 --> 00:41:33,200
as much as it needs, including
possible exploration,

850
00:41:33,200 --> 00:41:38,520
until it then tries to exploit
and give an explanation.

851
00:41:38,520 --> 00:41:42,880
And so this paper here was
a paper that explored that.

852
00:41:42,880 --> 00:41:47,400
They found that when they were
trying to explicitly optimize

853
00:41:47,400 --> 00:41:50,780
for effective use of
test-time compute,

854
00:41:50,780 --> 00:41:53,820
they were able to get higher
performance with the same number

855
00:41:53,820 --> 00:41:58,100
of tokens, and also actually
reduce the number of tokens

856
00:41:58,100 --> 00:42:00,720
needed to get the same
performance so that you aren't,

857
00:42:00,720 --> 00:42:05,380
for example, using up a ton
of tokens on simple problems.

858
00:42:05,380 --> 00:42:08,220
So that's a use case
of meta-RL or ideas

859
00:42:08,220 --> 00:42:11,660
for meta-RL in language models.

860
00:42:11,660 --> 00:42:14,140
Now, a quick
digression, and this

861
00:42:14,140 --> 00:42:16,140
will actually lead us
into a different way

862
00:42:16,140 --> 00:42:18,540
of thinking about meta-RL.

863
00:42:18,540 --> 00:42:20,860
So we've talked about
multi-task policies

864
00:42:20,860 --> 00:42:23,100
where we condition
on a task descriptor.

865
00:42:23,100 --> 00:42:24,660
So maybe we want
a robot to stack

866
00:42:24,660 --> 00:42:26,180
in different
locations or a robot

867
00:42:26,180 --> 00:42:28,300
to walk in different directions.

868
00:42:28,300 --> 00:42:31,620
And we've talked about policies
that take as input experience

869
00:42:31,620 --> 00:42:35,878
and then generate actions
based off of that dataset.

870
00:42:35,878 --> 00:42:38,420
And if you were to think about
how this relates to multi-task

871
00:42:38,420 --> 00:42:40,740
learning policies, one
way to think about it

872
00:42:40,740 --> 00:42:43,920
is that we're just doing
multi-task learning,

873
00:42:43,920 --> 00:42:47,820
but now this data is
our task identifier.

874
00:42:47,820 --> 00:42:51,730
And this is something that
actually changes within a task.

875
00:42:51,730 --> 00:42:53,470
So the analogy is not perfect.

876
00:42:53,470 --> 00:42:55,650
It's not actually
fixed for a given task.

877
00:42:55,650 --> 00:42:58,510
You can think about it as
conditioning on the task,

878
00:42:58,510 --> 00:43:01,050
insofar as experience tells
you what the task you're

879
00:43:01,050 --> 00:43:03,010
trying to do is.

880
00:43:03,010 --> 00:43:04,690
Cool.

881
00:43:04,690 --> 00:43:07,930
And if you think about
goal-conditioned policies,

882
00:43:07,930 --> 00:43:10,810
rewards, you can
also view meta-RL

883
00:43:10,810 --> 00:43:13,410
as kind of a generalization
of goal-conditioned policies.

884
00:43:13,410 --> 00:43:17,970
Because there's a set of MDPs
that are goal-reaching MDPs.

885
00:43:17,970 --> 00:43:19,610
And then there's a
broader set of MDPs

886
00:43:19,610 --> 00:43:23,830
that are reward-maximizing MDPs.

887
00:43:23,830 --> 00:43:25,730
And then the other way
to think about this

888
00:43:25,730 --> 00:43:28,630
is instead of trying to
generalize to new goals,

889
00:43:28,630 --> 00:43:32,570
you can think about meta-RL as
trying to adapt to new tasks

890
00:43:32,570 --> 00:43:34,490
with a small amount
of experience.

891
00:43:34,490 --> 00:43:37,930
And this would be the difference
between zero-shot generalization

892
00:43:37,930 --> 00:43:40,810
and the goal-conditioned
case and k-shot

893
00:43:40,810 --> 00:43:43,770
generalization with k
episodes or k time steps

894
00:43:43,770 --> 00:43:45,880
in the meta-RL case.

895
00:43:45,880 --> 00:43:48,623

896
00:43:48,623 --> 00:43:50,040
So we've talked
about how we could

897
00:43:50,040 --> 00:43:54,900
view the experience as the
task descriptors [INAUDIBLE]

898
00:43:54,900 --> 00:43:56,733
the prompt, essentially.

899
00:43:56,733 --> 00:43:58,400
But there's actually
another way to look

900
00:43:58,400 --> 00:44:02,880
at it, which is that if we think
about this policy with memory

901
00:44:02,880 --> 00:44:07,160
that has the past memory
and then our current policy,

902
00:44:07,160 --> 00:44:09,600
if we were to think about what
information is being passed

903
00:44:09,600 --> 00:44:14,800
right here to the policy;
this information is presumably

904
00:44:14,800 --> 00:44:17,600
information about the task.

905
00:44:17,600 --> 00:44:21,340
And specifically when zi
isn't part of the state,

906
00:44:21,340 --> 00:44:24,500
there's ambiguity about what
MDP you're trying to solve.

907
00:44:24,500 --> 00:44:27,800
And you're using that
experience to try to identify

908
00:44:27,800 --> 00:44:30,840
the MDP that you're in.

909
00:44:30,840 --> 00:44:32,680
And you can think
about it as the policy

910
00:44:32,680 --> 00:44:34,740
needs to infer the task.

911
00:44:34,740 --> 00:44:37,240
It needs to infer specifically
some sort of latent task

912
00:44:37,240 --> 00:44:40,400
descriptor from its experience.

913
00:44:40,400 --> 00:44:42,480
And so another way
of looking at meta-RL

914
00:44:42,480 --> 00:44:45,880
is something where you
are using your experience

915
00:44:45,880 --> 00:44:50,100
to infer what the task
is, and then conditioning

916
00:44:50,100 --> 00:44:52,800
your policy that's acting
at the current time

917
00:44:52,800 --> 00:44:58,577
step on your current
estimate of what the task is.

918
00:44:58,577 --> 00:45:00,160
So you can think
about it essentially,

919
00:45:00,160 --> 00:45:04,060
as meta-RL is trying to explore,
to infer this unknown task,

920
00:45:04,060 --> 00:45:06,700
and then perform the task.

921
00:45:06,700 --> 00:45:07,240
I see.

922
00:45:07,240 --> 00:45:10,060
So you're asking, because we're
going to be inferring the task

923
00:45:10,060 --> 00:45:14,220
identifier, why don't we just
give it to the learning system?

924
00:45:14,220 --> 00:45:20,300
Yeah, so the main
reason is that if we

925
00:45:20,300 --> 00:45:22,900
don't have a compact
way of describing

926
00:45:22,900 --> 00:45:26,820
the task that the
agent is doing, then

927
00:45:26,820 --> 00:45:30,047
it may actually be challenging.

928
00:45:30,047 --> 00:45:31,880
If we don't have a good
way to represent it,

929
00:45:31,880 --> 00:45:34,420
then we're not sure what
to pass into the policy.

930
00:45:34,420 --> 00:45:36,787
And if we don't have a good
way of representing it,

931
00:45:36,787 --> 00:45:38,620
we can always resort
to just using an index,

932
00:45:38,620 --> 00:45:41,200
saying this is task 1, this
is task 2, this is task 3.

933
00:45:41,200 --> 00:45:42,980
But if you do that
and then at test time

934
00:45:42,980 --> 00:45:45,780
you say this is
task 100, it's not

935
00:45:45,780 --> 00:45:48,050
going to be able to
generalize in that scenario

936
00:45:48,050 --> 00:45:51,250
and do anything about that task.

937
00:45:51,250 --> 00:45:53,472
Whereas, conditioning
on experience

938
00:45:53,472 --> 00:45:54,930
is something that
could generalize,

939
00:45:54,930 --> 00:46:00,010
where it can actually be able
to use the more continuous space

940
00:46:00,010 --> 00:46:02,730
of states, rewards,
and so forth in order

941
00:46:02,730 --> 00:46:04,410
to figure out a new task.

942
00:46:04,410 --> 00:46:06,330
So it comes down
to representations.

943
00:46:06,330 --> 00:46:09,970
And you could view this as
learning a z representation,

944
00:46:09,970 --> 00:46:12,530
like learning like a
continuous vector that's

945
00:46:12,530 --> 00:46:15,090
representing our tasks.

946
00:46:15,090 --> 00:46:23,410
Now, in multi-task RL,
we talked about how

947
00:46:23,410 --> 00:46:26,170
if you formulate
some state space,

948
00:46:26,170 --> 00:46:28,450
that it corresponds
to the original state

949
00:46:28,450 --> 00:46:32,810
space and the z vector.

950
00:46:32,810 --> 00:46:35,690
Then, you get this
aggregate MDP.

951
00:46:35,690 --> 00:46:43,050
And so the result of this
is still an MDP, just with

952
00:46:43,050 --> 00:46:45,920
a new state space
and new dynamics

953
00:46:45,920 --> 00:46:48,480
to account for this
new state space.

954
00:46:48,480 --> 00:46:53,200
And so there's a question
of, for meta-RL, is there

955
00:46:53,200 --> 00:46:54,750
an aggregate MDP?

956
00:46:54,750 --> 00:47:01,040

957
00:47:01,040 --> 00:47:05,160
And this would be for meta-RL.

958
00:47:05,160 --> 00:47:08,200

959
00:47:08,200 --> 00:47:14,120
And in this case, we're not
providing zi in the state.

960
00:47:14,120 --> 00:47:18,880
And so actually,
inherently, it's

961
00:47:18,880 --> 00:47:20,100
actually partially observed.

962
00:47:20,100 --> 00:47:22,060
You don't actually
know what the task is.

963
00:47:22,060 --> 00:47:25,480
You're trying to explore and
figure that out from experience.

964
00:47:25,480 --> 00:47:28,960
And so there isn't
actually an aggregate

965
00:47:28,960 --> 00:47:30,920
MDP that you can think about.

966
00:47:30,920 --> 00:47:34,240
Instead, if you wanted
to try to aggregate it

967
00:47:34,240 --> 00:47:36,000
into a single
decision process, it

968
00:47:36,000 --> 00:47:40,640
would have to be a partially
observable MDP, or POMDP.

969
00:47:40,640 --> 00:47:45,566
And it's actually a
special kind of POMDP,

970
00:47:45,566 --> 00:47:47,140
where we talked
about before, where

971
00:47:47,140 --> 00:47:50,420
you have some partially
observable Markov decision

972
00:47:50,420 --> 00:47:57,660
process where you can't actually
observe S. And you have actions.

973
00:47:57,660 --> 00:48:03,660
And instead of observing S,
you observe some observations

974
00:48:03,660 --> 00:48:09,148
that are some function of S.

975
00:48:09,148 --> 00:48:12,180
That would be the
general POMDP case.

976
00:48:12,180 --> 00:48:17,700
One thing that's a little bit
special about the meta-RL thing

977
00:48:17,700 --> 00:48:21,740
is the only thing that you can't
observe is what the task is.

978
00:48:21,740 --> 00:48:27,140
And so it's basically a
POMDP where this hidden state

979
00:48:27,140 --> 00:48:35,820
space can be defined as the
observation and the task

980
00:48:35,820 --> 00:48:37,460
identifier.

981
00:48:37,460 --> 00:48:41,000
And if you can infer the task
identifier from experience,

982
00:48:41,000 --> 00:48:42,890
then you can essentially
view it as a way

983
00:48:42,890 --> 00:48:46,930
of trying to infer the
underlying state space as a way

984
00:48:46,930 --> 00:48:50,690
to solve the POMDP.

985
00:48:50,690 --> 00:48:52,850
So this is basically
another way of looking

986
00:48:52,850 --> 00:48:54,850
at the meta-RL
problem, as basically

987
00:48:54,850 --> 00:48:58,530
like a partially observable
Markov decision process.

988
00:48:58,530 --> 00:49:02,490
Now, there's a meta-RL method
that we actually talked about.

989
00:49:02,490 --> 00:49:06,610
I showed an architecture diagram
that was actually explicitly

990
00:49:06,610 --> 00:49:11,730
trying to output z's, And it
was actually outputting some

991
00:49:11,730 --> 00:49:13,058
distribution over z's.

992
00:49:13,058 --> 00:49:14,850
Because at the very
beginning, you actually

993
00:49:14,850 --> 00:49:15,930
don't know what z is.

994
00:49:15,930 --> 00:49:18,430
You have some kind of prior
belief over what z is.

995
00:49:18,430 --> 00:49:20,110
And then as you get
more experience,

996
00:49:20,110 --> 00:49:22,370
you're going to have a better
and better estimate of what z

997
00:49:22,370 --> 00:49:22,870
is.

998
00:49:22,870 --> 00:49:24,453
And so if you represent
a distribution

999
00:49:24,453 --> 00:49:28,330
over this kind of latent
task representation,

1000
00:49:28,330 --> 00:49:32,630
then you can do cool
things with that.

1001
00:49:32,630 --> 00:49:35,130
And we'll see that in a bit.

1002
00:49:35,130 --> 00:49:37,810
So this particular algorithm is
looking at continuous control

1003
00:49:37,810 --> 00:49:40,570
problems where different
tasks correspond

1004
00:49:40,570 --> 00:49:42,420
to walking in
different directions,

1005
00:49:42,420 --> 00:49:45,160
running at different velocities;
and also different physical

1006
00:49:45,160 --> 00:49:47,480
dynamics-- so being able to
adapt when the friction is

1007
00:49:47,480 --> 00:49:52,520
different or the inertia of
the robot system is different.

1008
00:49:52,520 --> 00:49:54,960
The other thing was
that everything,

1009
00:49:54,960 --> 00:49:58,840
what we saw before was using
a PPO-style algorithm, which

1010
00:49:58,840 --> 00:50:02,720
is somewhat off-policy
but not very off-policy.

1011
00:50:02,720 --> 00:50:05,600
It's more on-policy than
off-policy in some ways,

1012
00:50:05,600 --> 00:50:10,000
because it's not using a
replay buffer of past data.

1013
00:50:10,000 --> 00:50:12,380
And with meta-training
algorithms,

1014
00:50:12,380 --> 00:50:14,360
you can adapt really,
really quickly.

1015
00:50:14,360 --> 00:50:16,520
But the training process
might be really slow

1016
00:50:16,520 --> 00:50:21,560
if your base algorithm
is fairly on-policy.

1017
00:50:21,560 --> 00:50:26,440
And so as a question,
hopefully, this

1018
00:50:26,440 --> 00:50:28,760
should be a
straightforward question,

1019
00:50:28,760 --> 00:50:33,040
if your base algorithm for
updating the policy at step

1020
00:50:33,040 --> 00:50:35,500
four, if that's an
off-policy algorithm,

1021
00:50:35,500 --> 00:50:41,260
do you expect that to be more
or less efficient than if you

1022
00:50:41,260 --> 00:50:44,860
used an on-policy-based method?

1023
00:50:44,860 --> 00:50:45,800
Yeah, more efficient.

1024
00:50:45,800 --> 00:50:48,100
So if you're using
a replay buffer

1025
00:50:48,100 --> 00:50:52,982
and using that replay buffer to
update your policy with memory,

1026
00:50:52,982 --> 00:50:54,940
that should be a lot more
data efficient during

1027
00:50:54,940 --> 00:50:57,220
this meta-training process.

1028
00:50:57,220 --> 00:50:59,280
At test time, when
you're adapting,

1029
00:50:59,280 --> 00:51:01,000
the efficiency is
going to be similar.

1030
00:51:01,000 --> 00:51:03,100
So this is just talking
about the efficiency

1031
00:51:03,100 --> 00:51:08,380
for actually training this
in-context learning system.

1032
00:51:08,380 --> 00:51:13,420
And so if you actually look at
the curves over this training

1033
00:51:13,420 --> 00:51:17,420
process right here, the amount
of data that you need in

1034
00:51:17,420 --> 00:51:20,560
order to meta-learn in
these kinds of systems.

1035
00:51:20,560 --> 00:51:22,020
You can see that
in blue, if we're

1036
00:51:22,020 --> 00:51:26,637
using an off-policy algorithm
with a replay buffer,

1037
00:51:26,637 --> 00:51:28,220
it's a lot more data
efficient, learns

1038
00:51:28,220 --> 00:51:35,020
a lot more quickly
than RL2 and ProMP.

1039
00:51:35,020 --> 00:51:38,850
I want to say all three
of these are using base RL

1040
00:51:38,850 --> 00:51:41,850
algorithms that are more
on-policy and more like PPO,

1041
00:51:41,850 --> 00:51:43,490
for example.

1042
00:51:43,490 --> 00:51:46,090
Yeah, so the question is, can I
elaborate on what specifically

1043
00:51:46,090 --> 00:51:47,110
is in the replay buffer?

1044
00:51:47,110 --> 00:51:52,490

1045
00:51:52,490 --> 00:51:59,740
Well, so each replay buffer will
have a sequence of episodes.

1046
00:51:59,740 --> 00:52:01,490
Basically, each thing
in the replay buffer

1047
00:52:01,490 --> 00:52:05,130
will be a sequence of
episodes from a given MDP.

1048
00:52:05,130 --> 00:52:12,450
And that includes the states,
the actions, the rewards.

1049
00:52:12,450 --> 00:52:15,625
And when you optimize
this, oftentimes, you'll

1050
00:52:15,625 --> 00:52:17,250
have a different
replay buffer per task

1051
00:52:17,250 --> 00:52:20,610
so that you can sample a
task here and then optimize

1052
00:52:20,610 --> 00:52:28,813
the reward for that task using
the memory from that trajectory.

1053
00:52:28,813 --> 00:52:30,730
Typically, the buffer,
instead of just storing

1054
00:52:30,730 --> 00:52:32,897
states and actions, you're
storing full trajectories

1055
00:52:32,897 --> 00:52:36,420
together because you're going to
be training a model with memory.

1056
00:52:36,420 --> 00:52:40,960
And you need to be able to
provide that trajectory as input

1057
00:52:40,960 --> 00:52:43,320
to the policy.

1058
00:52:43,320 --> 00:52:44,160
Cool.

1059
00:52:44,160 --> 00:52:49,360
So summary so far, we've talked
about using these models to be

1060
00:52:49,360 --> 00:52:51,800
able to adapt to new MDPs.

1061
00:52:51,800 --> 00:52:52,500
They're general.

1062
00:52:52,500 --> 00:52:54,800
They're quite expressive.

1063
00:52:54,800 --> 00:52:59,200
And there's also a variety of
choices in the architecture.

1064
00:52:59,200 --> 00:53:01,800
One thing is they're
hard to optimize.

1065
00:53:01,800 --> 00:53:04,080
Because actually it
needs to figure out

1066
00:53:04,080 --> 00:53:06,300
how to adapt from data.

1067
00:53:06,300 --> 00:53:08,800
And there's lots of
different input features

1068
00:53:08,800 --> 00:53:10,900
here that you could try
to pay attention to.

1069
00:53:10,900 --> 00:53:15,440
And the model has to figure out
what about those input videos

1070
00:53:15,440 --> 00:53:18,000
that you're passing as input
for the maze navigation

1071
00:53:18,000 --> 00:53:21,360
is useful when you're then
trying to exploit and learn

1072
00:53:21,360 --> 00:53:23,337
a policy.

1073
00:53:23,337 --> 00:53:25,420
Because you're passing in
so many things as input,

1074
00:53:25,420 --> 00:53:27,600
it's such a big model, it
can be hard to optimize

1075
00:53:27,600 --> 00:53:28,540
for that reason.

1076
00:53:28,540 --> 00:53:30,832
And it can also be hard to
optimize from an exploration

1077
00:53:30,832 --> 00:53:33,200
standpoint, which we'll
talk about right after this.

1078
00:53:33,200 --> 00:53:37,500
And then the sample efficiency
of the meta-learning process

1079
00:53:37,500 --> 00:53:41,580
is going to be inherited from
whatever optimizer you use

1080
00:53:41,580 --> 00:53:44,100
in this last step right here.

1081
00:53:44,100 --> 00:53:47,280
And so if you use something
like vanilla policy gradient,

1082
00:53:47,280 --> 00:53:49,080
it'll be extremely
data inefficient.

1083
00:53:49,080 --> 00:53:50,860
If you use something
like PPO, it'll

1084
00:53:50,860 --> 00:53:54,760
be more stable, but
still fairly inefficient.

1085
00:53:54,760 --> 00:53:56,780
If you use something
like SAC, it

1086
00:53:56,780 --> 00:53:58,880
will be probably a
lot more efficient,

1087
00:53:58,880 --> 00:54:02,300
although you might need to
tune the hyperparameters more.

1088
00:54:02,300 --> 00:54:05,100
So now let's talk a
bit about exploration.

1089
00:54:05,100 --> 00:54:08,180
By exploration, I
mean exploration

1090
00:54:08,180 --> 00:54:11,900
in context,
exploration when you're

1091
00:54:11,900 --> 00:54:15,165
trying to explore a new task.

1092
00:54:15,165 --> 00:54:16,540
What we've talked
about so far is

1093
00:54:16,540 --> 00:54:19,660
trying to optimize
end-to-end for exploration

1094
00:54:19,660 --> 00:54:25,595
and exploitation, with respect
to the final sum of rewards.

1095
00:54:25,595 --> 00:54:27,220
Simple, in principle,
it could actually

1096
00:54:27,220 --> 00:54:29,547
yield the optimal
exploration-exploitation

1097
00:54:29,547 --> 00:54:31,380
trade-off because you're
optimizing directly

1098
00:54:31,380 --> 00:54:33,770
for the sum of rewards.

1099
00:54:33,770 --> 00:54:35,610
And that's really, really nice.

1100
00:54:35,610 --> 00:54:38,090
But it can be a really
challenging optimization.

1101
00:54:38,090 --> 00:54:40,570
And let's talk about
why that's the case.

1102
00:54:40,570 --> 00:54:45,150
So let's look at
a running example.

1103
00:54:45,150 --> 00:54:51,730
So say that you have some
robot that looks like this.

1104
00:54:51,730 --> 00:54:56,930
And the robot needs
to find something.

1105
00:54:56,930 --> 00:55:02,170
And there are some
hallways that the robot

1106
00:55:02,170 --> 00:55:05,490
might need to go
down in order to try

1107
00:55:05,490 --> 00:55:09,770
to find the thing that
it's trying to find.

1108
00:55:09,770 --> 00:55:13,370
And different tasks correspond
to different locations

1109
00:55:13,370 --> 00:55:15,310
of the object that
it's trying to find.

1110
00:55:15,310 --> 00:55:18,353
So in one task or
in one scenario,

1111
00:55:18,353 --> 00:55:19,770
it might be all
the way over here.

1112
00:55:19,770 --> 00:55:25,290
In another scenario, it might
be over here, and so forth.

1113
00:55:25,290 --> 00:55:28,930
And to help the robot
out a little bit,

1114
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:31,010
we're going to introduce
one other thing, which

1115
00:55:31,010 --> 00:55:35,280
is that there's going to be
some piece of paper over here.

1116
00:55:35,280 --> 00:55:43,360
And that paper actually says
where the object is in words.

1117
00:55:43,360 --> 00:55:46,582
Maybe it's trying to find
a replacement light bulb

1118
00:55:46,582 --> 00:55:47,540
or something like that.

1119
00:55:47,540 --> 00:55:50,680
And it says, the light bulb
is in the fourth hallway

1120
00:55:50,680 --> 00:55:53,320
at the end.

1121
00:55:53,320 --> 00:55:55,180
OK, so here's our robot.

1122
00:55:55,180 --> 00:55:56,853
Here are the hallways.

1123
00:55:56,853 --> 00:55:58,520
Different tasks
correspond to navigating

1124
00:55:58,520 --> 00:56:00,080
to the ends of the
different halls.

1125
00:56:00,080 --> 00:56:02,380
And we have some information
about where to go.

1126
00:56:02,380 --> 00:56:05,720
And if we look at
that, information

1127
00:56:05,720 --> 00:56:11,600
will be in the state about where
the reward is for this task.

1128
00:56:11,600 --> 00:56:15,020
Now, how do we actually
learn how to explore?

1129
00:56:15,020 --> 00:56:18,600
So say that we were trying
to collect trajectories

1130
00:56:18,600 --> 00:56:22,400
and trying to learn
how to explore such

1131
00:56:22,400 --> 00:56:26,130
that we can get good behavior.

1132
00:56:26,130 --> 00:56:28,790

1133
00:56:28,790 --> 00:56:33,040
Let's start with the scenario
where we sampled a task.

1134
00:56:33,040 --> 00:56:37,780
And maybe the task was to go to
the end of this hallway right

1135
00:56:37,780 --> 00:56:39,300
here.

1136
00:56:39,300 --> 00:56:44,400
And say that we
actually just got lucky.

1137
00:56:44,400 --> 00:56:47,540
We collected a trajectory
that just went straight

1138
00:56:47,540 --> 00:56:51,003
to that reward.

1139
00:56:51,003 --> 00:56:52,420
Now, in some ways,
that seems good

1140
00:56:52,420 --> 00:56:55,460
because we got reward for that.

1141
00:56:55,460 --> 00:56:59,580
And so that gave us some reward.

1142
00:56:59,580 --> 00:57:01,420
And that's optimizing
the objective

1143
00:57:01,420 --> 00:57:04,282
that we care about ultimately.

1144
00:57:04,282 --> 00:57:05,740
But the thing that's
bad about this

1145
00:57:05,740 --> 00:57:10,600
is that the robot got lucky.

1146
00:57:10,600 --> 00:57:14,300
And it doesn't actually know how
to repeat that in the future,

1147
00:57:14,300 --> 00:57:19,540
because it didn't actually
explore any other alternatives.

1148
00:57:19,540 --> 00:57:23,000
And so the way that
we can view this

1149
00:57:23,000 --> 00:57:25,000
is if it goes to the end
of the correct hallway,

1150
00:57:25,000 --> 00:57:26,560
it'll get positive reward.

1151
00:57:26,560 --> 00:57:31,450
But the context leading up to
getting that positive reward

1152
00:57:31,450 --> 00:57:34,450
doesn't actually look
different than any other task.

1153
00:57:34,450 --> 00:57:36,090
It went through here.

1154
00:57:36,090 --> 00:57:39,450
And there's nothing
that differentiates

1155
00:57:39,450 --> 00:57:42,490
what it did there, which was
like zero reward, zero reward,

1156
00:57:42,490 --> 00:57:45,250
than if the task, for
example, had been to go here

1157
00:57:45,250 --> 00:57:47,250
or to go here.

1158
00:57:47,250 --> 00:57:51,030
And as a result, it didn't
actually learn how to explore

1159
00:57:51,030 --> 00:57:54,410
and it didn't learn how to use
exploration information in order

1160
00:57:54,410 --> 00:57:55,850
to solve the task.

1161
00:57:55,850 --> 00:57:57,570
And so this trajectory
might not actually

1162
00:57:57,570 --> 00:58:02,693
be very helpful for
learning how to explore

1163
00:58:02,693 --> 00:58:03,860
and learning how to exploit.

1164
00:58:03,860 --> 00:58:06,490

1165
00:58:06,490 --> 00:58:11,930
Now, let's say that we had
a different trajectory.

1166
00:58:11,930 --> 00:58:13,490
So we're exploring.

1167
00:58:13,490 --> 00:58:17,850
And let's say that the
agent, instead of doing that,

1168
00:58:17,850 --> 00:58:19,930
it maybe sampled the same task.

1169
00:58:19,930 --> 00:58:22,690
And it went to the
end of this trajectory

1170
00:58:22,690 --> 00:58:24,690
and then continued,
didn't see that,

1171
00:58:24,690 --> 00:58:29,640
and then went to the
end of this trajectory.

1172
00:58:29,640 --> 00:58:37,960
So that would be good, in the
sense that it has experience

1173
00:58:37,960 --> 00:58:40,440
that ruled out one task.

1174
00:58:40,440 --> 00:58:42,662
And then it got rewarded
for a different task.

1175
00:58:42,662 --> 00:58:44,120
And so it's going
to be teaching it

1176
00:58:44,120 --> 00:58:47,480
something about an exploration
strategy that works.

1177
00:58:47,480 --> 00:58:50,538
The downside is that exploration
strategy is quite suboptimal.

1178
00:58:50,538 --> 00:58:53,080
Because it would have been a
lot more efficient to first look

1179
00:58:53,080 --> 00:58:57,720
at the instructions and then
go to the correct position.

1180
00:58:57,720 --> 00:59:01,300
And so it's somewhat
good, somewhat bad.

1181
00:59:01,300 --> 00:59:03,840
It provides signal on a
suboptimal exploration

1182
00:59:03,840 --> 00:59:06,120
and exploitation strategy.

1183
00:59:06,120 --> 00:59:10,360
And so it's perhaps better
than the first trajectory

1184
00:59:10,360 --> 00:59:13,110
that it did, but
still not ideal.

1185
00:59:13,110 --> 00:59:15,880

1186
00:59:15,880 --> 00:59:19,000
Now, let's consider
a third trajectory.

1187
00:59:19,000 --> 00:59:24,020
Say that we are again collecting
data for the meta-RL process.

1188
00:59:24,020 --> 00:59:27,420
And let's say that
the agent decided

1189
00:59:27,420 --> 00:59:31,620
to look at the instructions
and then just stay and watch

1190
00:59:31,620 --> 00:59:34,620
the instructions.

1191
00:59:34,620 --> 00:59:36,860
Does anyone have
thoughts on whether that

1192
00:59:36,860 --> 00:59:42,740
would be a useful trajectory or
not for the learning process?

1193
00:59:42,740 --> 00:59:43,720
Yeah, exactly.

1194
00:59:43,720 --> 00:59:46,020
So it did something
useful, but it doesn't

1195
00:59:46,020 --> 00:59:47,060
get any reward for that.

1196
00:59:47,060 --> 00:59:50,780
It only gets reward if it
did that and then went here.

1197
00:59:50,780 --> 00:59:54,740
And so that's also not going to
be very useful for the learning

1198
00:59:54,740 --> 00:59:55,900
problem.

1199
00:59:55,900 --> 01:00:00,340
And basically, in the
problem like this,

1200
01:00:00,340 --> 01:00:03,780
you're only going to actually
be learning something

1201
01:00:03,780 --> 01:00:08,460
useful about how to explore and
exploit if you successfully did

1202
01:00:08,460 --> 01:00:11,100
the proper exploration behavior
and then did the exploitation

1203
01:00:11,100 --> 01:00:11,840
behavior.

1204
01:00:11,840 --> 01:00:15,585
And if you do them
in separate episodes,

1205
01:00:15,585 --> 01:00:18,980
then you're not actually
getting good learning signal.

1206
01:00:18,980 --> 01:00:21,740
And that means that you have
this kind of sample complexity

1207
01:00:21,740 --> 01:00:25,010
where you need to actually get
lucky and do both of the two

1208
01:00:25,010 --> 01:00:28,370
things at the same time in
order to get signal that

1209
01:00:28,370 --> 01:00:31,752
helps you learn the problem.

1210
01:00:31,752 --> 01:00:34,210
So basically, it's hard to
learn how to explore and exploit

1211
01:00:34,210 --> 01:00:35,070
at the same time.

1212
01:00:35,070 --> 01:00:37,620

1213
01:00:37,620 --> 01:00:39,370
Another example of
this is say that you're

1214
01:00:39,370 --> 01:00:41,250
trying to learn how to cook.

1215
01:00:41,250 --> 01:00:43,150
You have data in
previous kitchens.

1216
01:00:43,150 --> 01:00:45,530
Your goal is to then be
able to quickly learn

1217
01:00:45,530 --> 01:00:48,092
how to cook in a new kitchen.

1218
01:00:48,092 --> 01:00:49,550
Now, in order to
learn how to cook,

1219
01:00:49,550 --> 01:00:51,330
you need to find
the ingredients.

1220
01:00:51,330 --> 01:00:53,777
And if you haven't
yet figured out

1221
01:00:53,777 --> 01:00:55,610
how to find the ingredients
because you have

1222
01:00:55,610 --> 01:00:57,690
bad exploration,
then you can't learn

1223
01:00:57,690 --> 01:01:00,790
how to cook because you don't
have the ingredients with you.

1224
01:01:00,790 --> 01:01:04,630
And then you're going
to get a low reward.

1225
01:01:04,630 --> 01:01:06,130
And then also, if
you don't yet know

1226
01:01:06,130 --> 01:01:08,890
how to cook because you
haven't collected experience

1227
01:01:08,890 --> 01:01:11,990
on that, then regardless
of the exploration you do,

1228
01:01:11,990 --> 01:01:13,930
you're going to get low reward.

1229
01:01:13,930 --> 01:01:15,890
And so you have this
chicken and egg problem

1230
01:01:15,890 --> 01:01:18,450
between learning how
to explore and learning

1231
01:01:18,450 --> 01:01:21,610
how to solve the task
using that information.

1232
01:01:21,610 --> 01:01:26,000
And so there's another way to
view this coupling problem where

1233
01:01:26,000 --> 01:01:28,920
exploration and
execution or exploitation

1234
01:01:28,920 --> 01:01:32,600
depend on each other in order
to be jointly successful.

1235
01:01:32,600 --> 01:01:35,760
So then the way to view
end-to-end training

1236
01:01:35,760 --> 01:01:38,280
is that it's great
in that you can

1237
01:01:38,280 --> 01:01:39,800
learn this optimal trade-off.

1238
01:01:39,800 --> 01:01:41,680
But it can be
extremely challenging

1239
01:01:41,680 --> 01:01:44,120
if you have a hard exploration
problem, because you

1240
01:01:44,120 --> 01:01:46,920
need to figure out how to
explore and exploit and get

1241
01:01:46,920 --> 01:01:50,122
learning signal that way.

1242
01:01:50,122 --> 01:01:52,080
The question is, is the
ultimate challenge here

1243
01:01:52,080 --> 01:01:53,580
that the reward is sparse?

1244
01:01:53,580 --> 01:01:57,970
So it's true that if
you had a dense reward,

1245
01:01:57,970 --> 01:01:59,907
you'd be in a much
better place, basically,

1246
01:01:59,907 --> 01:02:01,740
because you wouldn't
really have to explore.

1247
01:02:01,740 --> 01:02:08,170

1248
01:02:08,170 --> 01:02:09,920
Well, you wouldn't
have to explicitly take

1249
01:02:09,920 --> 01:02:10,720
actions to explore.

1250
01:02:10,720 --> 01:02:13,360

1251
01:02:13,360 --> 01:02:15,480
And this really comes
down to a problem

1252
01:02:15,480 --> 01:02:16,960
when exploration is hard.

1253
01:02:16,960 --> 01:02:19,320
If exploration is easy,
this really isn't a problem.

1254
01:02:19,320 --> 01:02:21,580
Because you no longer have
to solve both exploration

1255
01:02:21,580 --> 01:02:22,440
and exploitation.

1256
01:02:22,440 --> 01:02:25,217
You only have to
solve exploitation.

1257
01:02:25,217 --> 01:02:26,800
So there's a couple
solutions to this.

1258
01:02:26,800 --> 01:02:29,780
And we'll start to talk
about one solution today.

1259
01:02:29,780 --> 01:02:34,860
And then on Friday, we'll
talk about more solutions.

1260
01:02:34,860 --> 01:02:39,460
So the second solution, I'm not
going to go into complete detail

1261
01:02:39,460 --> 01:02:40,480
on how these work.

1262
01:02:40,480 --> 01:02:44,020
But one solution here is,
instead of optimizing just

1263
01:02:44,020 --> 01:02:48,460
for end-to-end reward, you can
add in auxiliary exploration

1264
01:02:48,460 --> 01:02:53,540
objectives to encourage it
to try to actually figure out

1265
01:02:53,540 --> 01:02:57,460
something more meaningful
about the task.

1266
01:02:57,460 --> 01:02:59,900
And one approach
here is to use what's

1267
01:02:59,900 --> 01:03:04,900
called posterior sampling,
also called Thompson sampling.

1268
01:03:04,900 --> 01:03:11,020
And the way that
this works is that we

1269
01:03:11,020 --> 01:03:13,180
talked about an algorithm
where you're trying

1270
01:03:13,180 --> 01:03:15,580
to infer what the task is.

1271
01:03:15,580 --> 01:03:20,810
And if you've collected
some experience so

1272
01:03:20,810 --> 01:03:27,690
far and you've inferred some
distribution over your task--

1273
01:03:27,690 --> 01:03:30,830
in this case, zi is now
some latent representation,

1274
01:03:30,830 --> 01:03:34,610
it's not a representation
that's known--

1275
01:03:34,610 --> 01:03:38,410
then what you could
do is you could then

1276
01:03:38,410 --> 01:03:44,370
sample from this
distribution and then act as

1277
01:03:44,370 --> 01:03:47,610
if that was the correct task.

1278
01:03:47,610 --> 01:03:51,270
So if you have this
task inference procedure

1279
01:03:51,270 --> 01:03:55,930
and then you also have a policy
that, given an estimate of z,

1280
01:03:55,930 --> 01:04:03,370
is able to generate actions,
then what you can do

1281
01:04:03,370 --> 01:04:07,570
is you can sample a z from this
distribution and then sample

1282
01:04:07,570 --> 01:04:08,360
from this policy.

1283
01:04:08,360 --> 01:04:13,908

1284
01:04:13,908 --> 01:04:15,950
This starts to decouple
the problem a little bit.

1285
01:04:15,950 --> 01:04:22,280
Because if you have policies
that for a given z do the task,

1286
01:04:22,280 --> 01:04:25,920
then you would basically
learn an exploration strategy

1287
01:04:25,920 --> 01:04:36,560
that is trying to go down the
different corridors to find

1288
01:04:36,560 --> 01:04:37,260
the solution.

1289
01:04:37,260 --> 01:04:39,258
So as an example,
this is actually

1290
01:04:39,258 --> 01:04:41,800
a problem that looks somewhat
similar to the corridors, where

1291
01:04:41,800 --> 01:04:45,400
you get a sparse reward if you
reach one of these circles.

1292
01:04:45,400 --> 01:04:48,520
And the different circles
correspond to different tasks.

1293
01:04:48,520 --> 01:04:53,720
And in this case, we've
sampled this task right here.

1294
01:04:53,720 --> 01:05:02,040
And first, we sample a z
from our prior over tasks.

1295
01:05:02,040 --> 01:05:03,580
We haven't collected
any data yet.

1296
01:05:03,580 --> 01:05:04,780
So we sample z.

1297
01:05:04,780 --> 01:05:07,240
We then run our policy based
on those different z's.

1298
01:05:07,240 --> 01:05:09,520
And we get exploration
based on these.

1299
01:05:09,520 --> 01:05:13,667
And then we use that to
infer what the task might be.

1300
01:05:13,667 --> 01:05:15,500
And so now we know that
the task is probably

1301
01:05:15,500 --> 01:05:17,837
either over here or over here.

1302
01:05:17,837 --> 01:05:19,420
And so now you'll
collect trajectories

1303
01:05:19,420 --> 01:05:22,440
that are lighter in color that
are like this one, this one,

1304
01:05:22,440 --> 01:05:24,507
and going over here.

1305
01:05:24,507 --> 01:05:26,840
And then once you've actually
collected this trajectory,

1306
01:05:26,840 --> 01:05:29,257
then you should actually have
a much narrower distribution

1307
01:05:29,257 --> 01:05:31,740
over the task because you
get positive reward here.

1308
01:05:31,740 --> 01:05:37,120
And so then your z distribution
will be narrowed out here.

1309
01:05:37,120 --> 01:05:42,540
And you'll execute your
policy according to those z's.

1310
01:05:42,540 --> 01:05:47,140
And so that's one approach for
addressing this exploration

1311
01:05:47,140 --> 01:05:48,420
problem--

1312
01:05:48,420 --> 01:05:51,740
basically, learning this latent
representation over tasks

1313
01:05:51,740 --> 01:05:55,100
and then sampling
from that distribution

1314
01:05:55,100 --> 01:05:58,680
and acting according to
your policy for that task.

1315
01:05:58,680 --> 01:06:02,260

1316
01:06:02,260 --> 01:06:04,420
So there's some scenarios
where this is great

1317
01:06:04,420 --> 01:06:06,768
and scenarios where
this is not so great.

1318
01:06:06,768 --> 01:06:09,060
Does anyone have thoughts on
when this sort of sampling

1319
01:06:09,060 --> 01:06:11,500
might be bad, like
what sort of MDPs

1320
01:06:11,500 --> 01:06:13,930
this might not work well in?

1321
01:06:13,930 --> 01:06:16,370
"If you have a
large action space."

1322
01:06:16,370 --> 01:06:18,730
That could contribute.

1323
01:06:18,730 --> 01:06:21,930
Although, it isn't just having
to do with the action space.

1324
01:06:21,930 --> 01:06:24,430
Because if you have a dense
reward and a large action space,

1325
01:06:24,430 --> 01:06:26,370
you'd be probably OK.

1326
01:06:26,370 --> 01:06:27,870
So if these are out
of distribution,

1327
01:06:27,870 --> 01:06:29,715
certainly things
will be problematic.

1328
01:06:29,715 --> 01:06:31,090
But even in
distribution, there's

1329
01:06:31,090 --> 01:06:36,010
scenarios where this exploration
strategy will be really bad.

1330
01:06:36,010 --> 01:06:40,490
Yeah, so one scenario where
this might work poorly

1331
01:06:40,490 --> 01:06:47,610
is if you start to
see some reward,

1332
01:06:47,610 --> 01:06:49,770
but there might actually
be an even better reward

1333
01:06:49,770 --> 01:06:53,450
in another scenario,
you might start

1334
01:06:53,450 --> 01:06:55,690
to exploit a little
bit too early.

1335
01:06:55,690 --> 01:06:58,610
Although, it won't rule out
those higher reward scenarios

1336
01:06:58,610 --> 01:07:00,650
if it knows that
there are other tasks

1337
01:07:00,650 --> 01:07:03,050
that might have higher rewards.

1338
01:07:03,050 --> 01:07:05,755
So I guess the scenario
that I was thinking

1339
01:07:05,755 --> 01:07:07,130
of where I think
that this can be

1340
01:07:07,130 --> 01:07:11,370
quite bad is actually a lot
like this scenario right here.

1341
01:07:11,370 --> 01:07:13,480
So the strategy that
posterior sampling

1342
01:07:13,480 --> 01:07:15,520
will learn in this
scenario is, instead

1343
01:07:15,520 --> 01:07:17,165
of looking at the
instructions, it's

1344
01:07:17,165 --> 01:07:19,040
instead going to go down
each of the hallways

1345
01:07:19,040 --> 01:07:20,880
and try to find the reward.

1346
01:07:20,880 --> 01:07:23,200
Because it's trying
to explicitly explore

1347
01:07:23,200 --> 01:07:25,200
by executing policies
corresponding

1348
01:07:25,200 --> 01:07:26,920
to different tasks.

1349
01:07:26,920 --> 01:07:30,800
And this will be especially bad
if the hallways are very long.

1350
01:07:30,800 --> 01:07:34,120
So if the hallways are
really, really long,

1351
01:07:34,120 --> 01:07:36,800
take days to traverse,
then what it's going to do

1352
01:07:36,800 --> 01:07:39,160
is it's going to spend
days going down one of them

1353
01:07:39,160 --> 01:07:41,420
and then figuring out that
it's not the right one.

1354
01:07:41,420 --> 01:07:43,380
And then spend days
going down another one,

1355
01:07:43,380 --> 01:07:45,600
rather than trying to
seek out information that

1356
01:07:45,600 --> 01:07:49,100
might tell it about what to do.

1357
01:07:49,100 --> 01:07:51,818
So, for example, if these
goals are very far away

1358
01:07:51,818 --> 01:07:53,360
and there is some
sign that tells you

1359
01:07:53,360 --> 01:07:57,980
what the correct goal is,
then this is a bad strategy.

1360
01:07:57,980 --> 01:08:01,560
Now, if that sign doesn't
exist, then it actually

1361
01:08:01,560 --> 01:08:04,080
would be a good strategy,
because you have to go down

1362
01:08:04,080 --> 01:08:06,426
those different corridors.

1363
01:08:06,426 --> 01:08:08,120
There's a couple
of other approaches

1364
01:08:08,120 --> 01:08:10,660
that we can use as well
that include [INAUDIBLE]

1365
01:08:10,660 --> 01:08:13,780
intrinsic rewards
that try to encourage

1366
01:08:13,780 --> 01:08:17,939
it to visit new states, and also
trying to visit states that help

1367
01:08:17,939 --> 01:08:20,620
you distinguish the
dynamics and reward

1368
01:08:20,620 --> 01:08:23,020
models for different tasks.

1369
01:08:23,020 --> 01:08:26,060
So we try to collect data
so that our model of rewards

1370
01:08:26,060 --> 01:08:28,660
and states are accurate.

1371
01:08:28,660 --> 01:08:30,220
And with this sort
of approach, you

1372
01:08:30,220 --> 01:08:31,939
could actually get
exploration strategy

1373
01:08:31,939 --> 01:08:34,340
that looks a lot more efficient,
that looks something like this,

1374
01:08:34,340 --> 01:08:35,979
compared to posterior
sampling, that's

1375
01:08:35,979 --> 01:08:40,020
trying to just sample the
different trajectories

1376
01:08:40,020 --> 01:08:43,100
for the different tasks.

1377
01:08:43,100 --> 01:08:45,220
There's also scenarios
where this might be bad,

1378
01:08:45,220 --> 01:08:48,620
if there's lots of distractors
or really complex state

1379
01:08:48,620 --> 01:08:50,185
dynamics.

1380
01:08:50,185 --> 01:08:52,060
But unfortunately, I
don't have too much time

1381
01:08:52,060 --> 01:08:52,960
to go over that now.

1382
01:08:52,960 --> 01:08:54,620
So these sorts of
approaches are easier

1383
01:08:54,620 --> 01:08:56,160
to optimize than end-to-end.

1384
01:08:56,160 --> 01:08:58,580
A lot of them have some
principle behind them,

1385
01:08:58,580 --> 01:09:01,920
but they can be very suboptimal
in certain environments.

1386
01:09:01,920 --> 01:09:05,810
And it kind of depends on the
environment or the domain.

